window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "information-theory", "lessons": {"surprise": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-measure-information-at-all\" data-h=\"3\">Why measure information at all?</h3>\n<p>Every day you receive messages. Some tell you almost nothing, and some change your plans completely. \"The sun rose this morning\" carries no news, because you were already sure of it. \"It snowed in the Sahara\" is headline material, because almost nobody expected it. In 1948 the engineer Claude Shannon asked a precise question: can we put a <em>number</em> on how much information a message carries? His answer founded information theory, and the same number now sits at the heart of every classification loss, every compression program and every language model.</p>\n<p>The key idea is simple. Information is the same thing as <strong>surprise</strong>. A message is informative exactly to the extent that it was unlikely before you heard it. So the information in an event should depend only on its probability, and it should be large when the probability is small.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a friend is thinking of a whole number from 1 to 8, and you may only ask yes/no questions. The smart strategy is to halve the possibilities each time: \"Is it 5 or more?\" \"Is it 7 or more?\" and so on. After 3 questions you always know the number, because <span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span>. Each yes/no answer that splits the possibilities in half gives you one <strong>bit</strong> of information, and learning which of 8 equally likely options is true is worth 3 bits.</p>\n<p>Now suppose the number is from 1 to 1024. You need 10 questions, because <span class=\"m\">2^{10} = 1024</span>. Learning the answer is worth 10 bits. Notice the pattern: an outcome with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac18</span> is worth 3 bits, and an outcome with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{1024}</span> is worth 10 bits. In both cases the number of bits is the power of 2 in the denominator. That is a logarithm: <span class=\"m\">3 = \\log_2 8</span> and <span class=\"m\">10 = \\log_2 1024</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>For an event with probability <span class=\"m\">p</span>, Shannon defined its <strong>information content</strong>, also called its <strong>surprisal</strong>, as\n<span class=\"mb\">I(p) = \\log_2\\frac{1}{p} = -\\log_2 p \\quad\\text{bits.}</span>\nLet's test it on a few cases. A certain event has <span class=\"m\">p = 1</span>, and <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 1 = 0</span>: no surprise, no information. A fair coin landing heads has <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac12</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I = -\\log_2\\frac12 = 1</span> bit. Rolling a 6 on a fair die has <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac16</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I = \\log_2 6 \\approx 2.585</span> bits. Drawing the ace of spades from a shuffled deck has <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac{1}{52}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I = \\log_2 52 \\approx 5.70</span> bits. The rarer the event, the bigger the number, exactly as our intuition demanded.</p>\n<p>Probabilities that are not powers of <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> give fractional bits, and that is fine. An event with probability <span class=\"m\">0.9</span> carries only <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.9 \\approx 0.152</span> bits, while an event with probability <span class=\"m\">0.01</span> carries <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.01 \\approx 6.64</span> bits. A fractional bit simply means \"less informative than a fair coin flip\".</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Information content (surprisal)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The information carried by an event of probability <span class=\"m\">p</span>: <span class=\"m\">I(p) = -\\log_2 p</span> bits. It is 0 for a certain event and grows without bound as <span class=\"m\">p \\to 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bit</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The unit of information when logarithms are taken base 2. One bit is the information in the outcome of a fair coin flip, or in the answer to a yes/no question whose two answers were equally likely.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-a-logarithm-and-not-something-else\" data-h=\"3\">Why a logarithm and not something else?</h3>\n<p>You might wonder why Shannon chose a logarithm rather than, say, <span class=\"m\">\\frac1p</span>. The reason is how information should behave for <strong>independent</strong> events. Suppose you flip a fair coin and roll a fair die. Learning both results should give you the information of the coin <em>plus</em> the information of the die, since the two facts have nothing to do with each other. But the probability of the combined event multiplies: <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{heads and } 6) = \\frac12\\cdot\\frac16 = \\frac1{12}</span>.</p>\n<p>So we need a function that turns multiplication into addition: <span class=\"m\">I(pq) = I(p) + I(q)</span>. The logarithm is exactly that function, since <span class=\"m\">\\log(pq) = \\log p + \\log q</span>. Check it with our numbers: <span class=\"m\">1 + 2.585 = 3.585</span> bits, and <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 12 \\approx 3.585</span> bits. They agree. In fact the logarithm is the <em>only</em> well-behaved function with this property, which is proved in the Formulas &amp; theorems section.</p>\n<h3 id=\"units-bits-nats-and-bans\" data-h=\"3\">Units: bits, nats and bans</h3>\n<p>The base of the logarithm only sets the unit, like choosing between metres and feet. Base 2 gives <strong>bits</strong>, natural logarithms (base <span class=\"m\">e</span>) give <strong>nats</strong>, and base 10 gives <strong>bans</strong> (also called hartleys). Converting is a single multiplication: <span class=\"m\">1 \\text{ bit} = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span> nats, and <span class=\"m\">1</span> nat <span class=\"m\">= \\frac{1}{\\ln 2} \\approx 1.443</span> bits. Machine learning code usually uses natural logs, so the losses you see printed during training are in nats.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Nat</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The unit of information when natural logarithms are used: <span class=\"m\">I = -\\ln p</span>. One nat is about 1.443 bits.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"information-adds-up-over-many-events\" data-h=\"3\">Information adds up over many events</h3>\n<p>Because information adds for independent events, the total surprise of a long sequence is the sum of the surprises of its pieces. Three fair coin flips coming up heads, heads, heads has probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac18</span> and carries <span class=\"m\">1 + 1 + 1 = 3</span> bits. A machine learning model that assigns probabilities to a whole dataset of independent examples has a total surprise equal to the sum of <span class=\"m\">-\\log p</span> over the examples. That sum is called the <strong>negative log-likelihood</strong>, and making it small is how most models are trained. You will meet it again as cross-entropy.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Information is about <em>probability</em>, not about meaning or importance. A coin flip that decides a million-dollar bet still carries exactly 1 bit.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p</span> is never negative, because <span class=\"m\">0 &lt; p \\le 1</span> makes <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 p \\le 0</span>. If you get a negative surprise, you dropped the minus sign.</li>\n<li>An event with <span class=\"m\">p = 0</span> has infinite surprise. In code, <span class=\"m\">\\log 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">-\\infty</span>, which is why practical systems clip probabilities away from 0.</li>\n<li>Don't mix units. A value computed with <code>np.log</code> is in nats; divide by <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2</span> to get bits.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The information in an event of probability <span class=\"m\">p</span> is <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p</span> bits. Certain events carry nothing, a fair coin flip carries 1 bit, and each halving of the probability adds one more bit. The logarithm is forced on us by the requirement that information from independent events adds up. Next we average this surprise over all outcomes of a random variable, which gives the most important quantity in the subject: entropy.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"binary-entropy\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Slide P(heads) and read the surprise of heads, −log₂ p, in the readout.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set P(heads) to 0.5 and read the surprise of heads: exactly 1 bit.\n2. Lower it to 0.25, then 0.125. Watch the surprise go to 2 bits, then 3 bits.\n3. Push P(heads) toward 0.001 and see the surprise climb to about 10 bits.\n4. Push it toward 0.999 and see the surprise of heads fall toward 0.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> each halving of the probability adds exactly one bit, which is the logarithm at work. The curve itself is the <em>average</em> surprise of the coin (its entropy), which is the subject of the next lesson. The readout line is the surprise of a single outcome, the quantity defined in this article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">I(p) = -\\log_2 p\\ \\text{bits} = -\\ln p\\ \\text{nats}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(pq) = I(p) + I(q)\\quad\\text{for independent events}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">1\\ \\text{bit} = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693\\ \\text{nats}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The logarithm is the only measure of surprise</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">I(p)</span> is continuous on <span class=\"m\">(0, 1]</span>, decreasing, and satisfies <span class=\"m\">I(pq) = I(p) + I(q)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">p, q</span>, then <span class=\"m\">I(p) = -c\\log_2 p</span> for some constant <span class=\"m\">c &gt; 0</span>. Choosing <span class=\"m\">I(\\frac12) = 1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">c = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">p = 2^{-x}</span> with <span class=\"m\">x \\ge 0</span> and define <span class=\"m\">g(x) = I(2^{-x})</span>. The additivity rule becomes <span class=\"m\">g(x + y) = I(2^{-x}2^{-y}) = g(x) + g(y)</span>. A continuous function with <span class=\"m\">g(x + y) = g(x) + g(y)</span> must be linear: first <span class=\"m\">g(n x) = n g(x)</span> for whole numbers <span class=\"m\">n</span>, then <span class=\"m\">g(\\frac mn) = \\frac mn g(1)</span> for fractions, and continuity extends this to every real <span class=\"m\">x</span>. So <span class=\"m\">g(x) = c x</span> with <span class=\"m\">c = g(1) = I(\\frac12)</span>, and since <span class=\"m\">x = -\\log_2 p</span>, we get <span class=\"m\">I(p) = -c\\log_2 p</span>. Because <span class=\"m\">I</span> decreases, <span class=\"m\">c &gt; 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How much information is in drawing the ace of spades?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>There are 52 equally likely cards, so <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac{1}{52}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">I = -\\log_2\\frac1{52} = \\log_2 52 = \\frac{\\ln 52}{\\ln 2}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\ln 52 \\approx 3.951</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I \\approx 5.70</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Sanity check: <span class=\"m\">2^5 = 32 &lt; 52 &lt; 64 = 2^6</span>, so the answer must be between 5 and 6 bits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">A fair coin shows heads and a fair die shows 6. Total information?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Coin: <span class=\"m\">I = -\\log_2\\frac12 = 1</span> bit.</li>\n<li>Die: <span class=\"m\">I = -\\log_2\\frac16 = \\log_2 6 \\approx 2.585</span> bits.</li>\n<li>The events are independent, so information adds: <span class=\"m\">1 + 2.585 = 3.585</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Check directly: the joint probability is <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{12}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 12 \\approx 3.585</span> bits. Same answer.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convert 3 bits of surprise into nats</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>3 bits means <span class=\"m\">p = 2^{-3} = \\frac18</span>.</li>\n<li>In nats, <span class=\"m\">I = -\\ln\\frac18 = \\ln 8 = 3\\ln 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">3 \\times 0.693 \\approx 2.079</span> nats.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A password is 8 characters, each chosen uniformly at random from the 26 lowercase letters. (a) How many bits of information does an attacker need to learn it? (b) Compare with a 4-digit PIN.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) There are <span class=\"m\">26^8</span> equally likely passwords, so the information is <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 26^8 = 8\\log_2 26 \\approx 8 \\times 4.700 = 37.6</span> bits. An attacker guessing blindly needs about <span class=\"m\">2^{37.6}</span> tries in the worst case.\n(b) A PIN has <span class=\"m\">10^4</span> options: <span class=\"m\">4\\log_2 10 \\approx 4 \\times 3.322 = 13.3</span> bits. The password carries almost three times as many bits, which means about <span class=\"m\">2^{24}</span> (roughly 17 million) times as many possibilities.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The <strong>negative log-likelihood</strong> of a model is the total surprise it feels at the training data: <span class=\"m\">-\\sum_i \\log q(y_i)</span>. Training a classifier or a language model means making the real data as unsurprising as possible. When a training log prints \"loss 0.69\" for a binary classifier, it is reporting about 0.69 nats per example, which is exactly the surprise of a fair coin (<span class=\"m\">\\ln 2</span>): the model is still guessing.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Binary search is the yes/no game from the article: finding one item among <span class=\"m\">n</span> sorted items takes about <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n</span> comparisons because each comparison yields at most one bit. This is also why any comparison sort needs about <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n! \\approx n\\log_2 n</span> comparisons: it must learn which of the <span class=\"m\">n!</span> orderings is the right one.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>News:</strong> \"Man bites dog\" is news and \"dog bites man\" is not, because the first is far less probable.</li>\n<li><strong>Weather in a desert:</strong> a forecast of \"sunny\" in a place that is sunny 99% of days carries <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.99 \\approx 0.014</span> bits; a forecast of \"rain\" carries <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.01 \\approx 6.6</span> bits.</li>\n<li><strong>Security:</strong> password strength meters estimate bits of surprise. Every extra random character from a 26-letter alphabet adds about 4.7 bits.</li>\n</ul></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Information of an event with probability 1/16, in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\log_2\\frac1{16} = \\log_2 16 = 4</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Three fair coin flips all land heads. How many bits of information is that?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The probability is <span class=\"m\">\\frac18</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 8 = 3</span>. Equivalently, 1 bit per independent flip.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which outcome carries more information?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">An event with probability 0.9</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">An event with probability 0.1</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">They carry the same</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Rarer means more surprising: <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.1 \\approx 3.32</span> bits versus <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.9 \\approx 0.15</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Event A carries 2 bits and an independent event B carries 3 bits. How many bits does \"A and B\" carry?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>For independent events the probabilities multiply, so the logarithms add: <span class=\"m\">2 + 3 = 5</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many nats is one bit? (3 decimal places)</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.693|ln 2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One bit is <span class=\"m\">-\\ln\\frac12 = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span> nats.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How much information does an event with probability 1 carry?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 1 = 0</span>. You learn nothing from something you were already certain of.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "entropy": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"from-one-outcome-to-a-whole-distribution\" data-h=\"3\">From one outcome to a whole distribution</h3>\n<p>In the last lesson we measured the surprise of a single event. But usually we care about a <strong>source</strong> of events: a coin that will be flipped many times, a weather station reporting every day, a language model producing word after word. Before the next outcome arrives, we don't know which one it will be, so we can't know its exact surprise. What we <em>can</em> compute is the surprise we expect <strong>on average</strong>. That average is called <strong>entropy</strong>, and it measures how uncertain, unpredictable or \"spread out\" a random variable is.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine two towns. In Sunnyvale the forecast is \"sunny\" 9 days out of 10 and \"rain\" 1 day out of 10. In Coinflip City it is sunny or rainy with equal chance. Which town's forecast is more informative to hear each morning? In Sunnyvale you can almost guess the forecast without listening; the rare rainy day is very surprising, but it happens so seldom that, on average, the forecast tells you little. In Coinflip City every forecast settles a genuine 50/50 question. Entropy turns this intuition into a number.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>For a discrete random variable <span class=\"m\">X</span> with probabilities <span class=\"m\">p(x)</span>, the <strong>entropy</strong> is the expected surprise:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X) = \\sum_x p(x)\\,\\log_2\\frac1{p(x)} = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log_2 p(x).</span>\nEach outcome contributes its surprise <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p(x)</span>, weighted by how often it happens. Outcomes with <span class=\"m\">p(x) = 0</span> contribute nothing, since we use the convention <span class=\"m\">0\\log 0 = 0</span> (the limit of <span class=\"m\">p\\log p</span> as <span class=\"m\">p \\to 0</span> is 0).</p>\n<p>Let's compute the two towns. Coinflip City: <span class=\"m\">H = \\frac12\\cdot1 + \\frac12\\cdot1 = 1</span> bit. Sunnyvale: the sunny days have surprise <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.9 \\approx 0.152</span> and the rainy days <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.1 \\approx 3.322</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">H = 0.9(0.152) + 0.1(3.322) \\approx 0.137 + 0.332 = 0.469 \\text{ bits.}</span>\nSunnyvale's forecast carries less than half a bit per day on average, even though its rare rainy forecast is very surprising.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The average surprise of a random variable: <span class=\"m\">H(X) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log_2 p(x)</span>. It measures uncertainty in bits (or nats with natural logs).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"building-intuition-with-more-outcomes\" data-h=\"3\">Building intuition with more outcomes</h3>\n<p>Take a distribution over three kinds of weather: sunny <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>, cloudy <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span>, rainy <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span>. The surprises are 1, 2 and 2 bits, so <span class=\"m\">H = \\frac12(1) + \\frac14(2) + \\frac14(2) = 1.5</span> bits. If instead all three were equally likely, <span class=\"m\">H = \\log_2 3 \\approx 1.585</span> bits, slightly more. Spreading probability evenly always increases uncertainty.</p>\n<p>For four outcomes with probabilities <span class=\"m\">\\frac12, \\frac14, \\frac18, \\frac18</span>: <span class=\"m\">H = \\frac12(1) + \\frac14(2) + \\frac18(3) + \\frac18(3) = 1.75</span> bits. For a fair six-sided die, every outcome has surprise <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 6</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H = \\log_2 6 \\approx 2.585</span> bits. In general, a uniform distribution over <span class=\"m\">n</span> outcomes has entropy exactly <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-two-extremes\" data-h=\"3\">The two extremes</h3>\n<p>Entropy is never negative, because each term <span class=\"m\">-p\\log_2 p</span> is at least zero. It equals zero only when one outcome has probability 1, which is a variable with no uncertainty at all. At the other extreme, among all distributions on <span class=\"m\">n</span> outcomes, the <strong>uniform</strong> distribution has the most entropy, <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n</span>. So for any distribution on <span class=\"m\">n</span> outcomes,\n<span class=\"mb\">0 \\le H(X) \\le \\log_2 n.</span>\nA distribution \"halfway\" between, like <span class=\"m\">(\\frac12, \\frac14, \\frac14)</span>, sits in between: 1.5 bits out of a maximum of 1.585.</p>\n<p>For a coin with <span class=\"m\">P(\\text{heads}) = p</span>, the entropy is the <strong>binary entropy function</strong> <span class=\"m\">h(p) = -p\\log_2 p - (1 - p)\\log_2(1 - p)</span>. It is 0 at <span class=\"m\">p = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = 1</span>, rises to its maximum of 1 bit at <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac12</span>, and is symmetric: <span class=\"m\">h(0.1) = h(0.9) \\approx 0.469</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Binary entropy function</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The entropy of a coin with heads-probability <span class=\"m\">p</span>: <span class=\"m\">h(p) = -p\\log_2 p - (1-p)\\log_2(1-p)</span>, which peaks at 1 bit when <span class=\"m\">p = \\frac12</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"binary-entropy\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The binary entropy function h(p): zero for a certain coin, one bit for a fair coin.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"what-entropy-really-measures-questions-and-code-length\" data-h=\"3\">What entropy really measures: questions and code length</h3>\n<p>Here is the most useful interpretation. Entropy is the average number of yes/no questions you need to identify the outcome, if you ask cleverly. For <span class=\"m\">(\\frac12, \\frac14, \\frac18, \\frac18)</span> over outcomes A, B, C, D, ask \"Is it A?\" first. Half the time you are done after 1 question. Otherwise ask \"Is it B?\" and a quarter of the time you are done after 2. Otherwise one more question separates C from D, for 3 questions. The average is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1) + \\frac14(2) + \\frac14(3) = 1.75</span> questions, which is exactly the entropy.</p>\n<p>The same calculation describes <strong>compression</strong>. Give A the code <code>0</code>, B <code>10</code>, C <code>110</code> and D <code>111</code>, and the average code length is 1.75 bits per symbol instead of the 2 bits a fixed-length code would need. Shannon proved that no lossless code can beat the entropy on average, and that you can get as close to it as you like. That is why entropy is the fundamental limit of compression, a result we study properly in the coding lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Why should the <em>average</em> of <span class=\"m\">-\\log p</span> be the right measure? Because surprise is the \"cost\" of an outcome in bits, and a long sequence of <span class=\"m\">N</span> outcomes contains about <span class=\"m\">N p(x)</span> copies of each outcome <span class=\"m\">x</span>. The total cost is about <span class=\"m\">\\sum_x N p(x)(-\\log_2 p(x)) = N H(X)</span>. So entropy is the cost per symbol of describing a long stream from the source. Predictable sources are cheap to describe; unpredictable ones are expensive.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Entropy belongs to a <strong>distribution</strong>, not to a single outcome. \"The entropy of heads\" doesn't make sense; \"the surprise of heads\" does.</li>\n<li>Entropy doesn't care about the <em>values</em> of outcomes, only their probabilities. A die labelled 1–6 and one labelled 10–60 have the same entropy.</li>\n<li>Don't forget the minus sign or the weights: <span class=\"m\">H</span> is <span class=\"m\">-\\sum p\\log p</span>, not <span class=\"m\">-\\sum \\log p</span>.</li>\n<li>Bits and nats differ by a factor of <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2</span>. The maximum for a coin is 1 bit, which is <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span> nats.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Entropy <span class=\"m\">H(X) = -\\sum p\\log_2 p</span> is the average surprise of a random variable. It is 0 for a certain outcome and at most <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n</span>, reached by the uniform distribution. It equals the average number of clever yes/no questions needed to pin down the outcome, and the minimum average number of bits needed to compress it. Next we extend it to pairs of variables.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"binary-entropy\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A coin is most uncertain at p = 0.5 (1 bit) and certain at 0 or 1.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Put P(heads) at 0.5 and confirm the entropy is exactly 1 bit.\n2. Move to 0.1 and then 0.9. Both give about 0.469 bits, Sunnyvale's number from the article.\n3. Drag slowly from 0.5 toward 0 and notice the curve is flat near the top and steep near the edges.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> a slightly unfair coin (say 0.6) is still almost 1 bit (about 0.971), so small biases barely reduce uncertainty. Only strongly biased coins become predictable. The symmetry <span class=\"m\">h(p) = h(1 - p)</span> shows that entropy doesn't care which outcome is likely, only how lopsided the distribution is.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"entropy\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the p bars. Uniform has maximum entropy; a spike has zero.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set all four p sliders equal. The readout shows <span class=\"m\">H(p) = 2</span> bits, the maximum <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 4</span>.\n2. Make p1 large and the others tiny. Watch <span class=\"m\">H(p)</span> fall toward 0.\n3. Recreate <span class=\"m\">(\\frac12, \\frac14, \\frac18, \\frac18)</span> by setting p1 = 0.5, p2 = 0.25, p3 = p4 = 0.125 and check you get 1.75 bits.\n4. Ignore the q sliders for now; they are used in the cross-entropy and KL lessons.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every move that makes the bars more even raises the entropy, and every move that concentrates mass lowers it. This is the bound <span class=\"m\">0 \\le H \\le \\log_2 n</span> from the article made visible.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">H(X) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log_2 p(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">0 \\le H(X) \\le \\log_2 n,\\quad H = \\log_2 n \\iff X \\text{ uniform}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(p) = -p\\log_2 p - (1-p)\\log_2(1-p)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bounds on entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a random variable with <span class=\"m\">n</span> possible outcomes, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le H(X) \\le \\log_2 n</span>. The lower bound holds with equality exactly when one outcome is certain; the upper bound exactly when <span class=\"m\">X</span> is uniform.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lower bound: each term <span class=\"m\">-p\\log_2 p \\ge 0</span> for <span class=\"m\">0 \\le p \\le 1</span>, and it is 0 only when <span class=\"m\">p \\in \\{0, 1\\}</span>. Upper bound: <span class=\"m\">\\log_2</span> is concave, so by Jensen's inequality the average of a log is at most the log of the average:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X) = \\sum_x p(x)\\log_2\\frac{1}{p(x)} \\le \\log_2\\Big(\\sum_x p(x)\\cdot\\frac1{p(x)}\\Big) = \\log_2 n,</span>\nwhere the sum runs over the <span class=\"m\">n</span> outcomes. Equality in Jensen's inequality requires <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{p(x)}</span> to be the same for every outcome, which means <span class=\"m\">p</span> is uniform.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Shannon's source coding theorem (preview)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>No lossless code can compress symbols from <span class=\"m\">X</span> to fewer than <span class=\"m\">H(X)</span> bits per symbol on average, and by coding long blocks you can get arbitrarily close to <span class=\"m\">H(X)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Entropy of (1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/8)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Surprises: <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2\\frac12 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2\\frac14 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2\\frac18 = 3</span> (twice).</li>\n<li>Weight by probability: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1) + \\frac14(2) + \\frac18(3) + \\frac18(3)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">= 0.5 + 0.5 + 0.375 + 0.375 = 1.75</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Compare with the maximum <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 4 = 2</span> bits for 4 outcomes.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Entropy of a coin with P(heads) = 0.9</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.9 \\approx 0.152</span> and <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.1 \\approx 3.322</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H = 0.9(0.152) + 0.1(3.322) \\approx 0.137 + 0.332</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H \\approx 0.469</span> bits, less than half the uncertainty of a fair coin.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weather: sunny 1/2, cloudy 1/4, rainy 1/4, versus uniform</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H = \\frac12(1) + \\frac14(2) + \\frac14(2) = 1.5</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Uniform over 3 outcomes: <span class=\"m\">H = \\log_2 3 \\approx 1.585</span> bits.</li>\n<li>The uneven forecast is slightly more predictable, by about 0.085 bits per day.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A loaded die shows 6 with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> and each of 1–5 with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac1{10}</span>. Find its entropy and compare it with a fair die.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">H = -\\frac12\\log_2\\frac12 - 5\\cdot\\frac1{10}\\log_2\\frac1{10} = 0.5 + 0.5\\log_2 10 \\approx 0.5 + 0.5(3.322) = 2.161</span> bits. A fair die has <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 6 \\approx 2.585</span> bits, so loading the die removes about 0.42 bits of uncertainty per roll.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Decision trees</strong> pick the split that reduces label entropy the most (information gain, next lesson).</li>\n<li><strong>Prediction uncertainty:</strong> the entropy of a classifier's output distribution flags inputs it is unsure about; active learning asks humans to label the highest-entropy examples.</li>\n<li><strong>Entropy bonuses</strong> in reinforcement learning (for example in soft actor-critic) add <span class=\"m\">\\beta H(\\pi)</span> to the reward so the policy keeps exploring.</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum entropy modelling:</strong> when you know only some constraints, choose the distribution with the highest entropy that satisfies them. Softmax classifiers arise this way.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Entropy is the limit for every lossless compressor. English text has an entropy of roughly 1 to 1.5 bits per character once context is used, much less than the 8 bits per character of plain ASCII, which is why text files compress so well. Random data (already maximum entropy) can't be compressed at all.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Word games:</strong> a good first guess in Wordle is one whose colour feedback has the highest entropy, because it splits the remaining words most evenly and so tells you the most on average.</li>\n<li><strong>Twenty questions:</strong> 20 well-chosen yes/no questions can distinguish <span class=\"m\">2^{20} \\approx 1</span> million things, so 20 bits are enough for a very large dictionary.</li>\n</ul></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Entropy of a fair 4-sided die, in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Uniform over 4 outcomes: <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 4 = 2</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which distribution over 3 outcomes has the highest entropy?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">(1/3, 1/3, 1/3)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">(1/2, 1/4, 1/4)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">(1, 0, 0)</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The uniform distribution maximizes entropy; <span class=\"m\">(1, 0, 0)</span> has zero entropy.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Entropy of (1/2, 1/4, 1/4) in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1) + \\frac14(2) + \\frac14(2) = 1.5</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Entropy of a uniform distribution over 32 outcomes, in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\log_2 32 = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A coin with P(heads) = 1 has entropy:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">0 bits</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">1 bit</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">infinite</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>There is no uncertainty, so the average surprise is <span class=\"m\">1\\cdot\\log_2 1 = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Entropy of a fair coin in nats (3 decimals)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.693|ln 2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>In nats, <span class=\"m\">H = -2\\cdot\\frac12\\ln\\frac12 = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "joint-conditional-entropy": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-look-at-two-variables-together\" data-h=\"3\">Why look at two variables together?</h3>\n<p>Real questions almost always involve more than one variable. Does knowing the weather help you predict whether people carry umbrellas? Does the word \"free\" in an email help you predict whether it is spam? Does a patient's symptom help you predict the disease? In each case there is a variable you want to know (umbrella, spam, disease) and a variable you can observe (weather, a word, a symptom). Information theory gives three tools for this: the <strong>joint entropy</strong> of both variables, the <strong>conditional entropy</strong> of one given the other, and the difference between them, the <strong>information gain</strong>. Decision trees, one of the most widely used machine learning models, are built entirely out of these three quantities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you stand at a train station and watch people come in. Let <span class=\"m\">X</span> be the weather (rain or dry) and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> be whether a person carries an umbrella. Over many days you record this joint table of probabilities:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>umbrella</th>\n<th>no umbrella</th>\n<th>total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>rain</td>\n<td>0.20</td>\n<td>0.05</td>\n<td>0.25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dry</td>\n<td>0.15</td>\n<td>0.60</td>\n<td>0.75</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>total</td>\n<td>0.35</td>\n<td>0.65</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The row totals give the distribution of <span class=\"m\">X</span> alone, <span class=\"m\">p(\\text{rain}) = 0.25</span>, and the column totals give <span class=\"m\">Y</span> alone, <span class=\"m\">p(\\text{umbrella}) = 0.35</span>. These totals are called <strong>marginal</strong> distributions, because they are written in the margins.</p>\n<h3 id=\"joint-entropy-uncertainty-about-the-pair\" data-h=\"3\">Joint entropy: uncertainty about the pair</h3>\n<p>The <strong>joint entropy</strong> <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y)</span> is just the entropy of the pair <span class=\"m\">(X, Y)</span>, treated as one variable with four possible values:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) = -\\sum_{x}\\sum_{y} p(x, y)\\log_2 p(x, y).</span>\nFor the station table, the four probabilities are 0.20, 0.05, 0.15 and 0.60. Their surprises are about 2.322, 4.322, 2.737 and 0.737 bits, so\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) \\approx 0.2(2.322) + 0.05(4.322) + 0.15(2.737) + 0.6(0.737) \\approx 1.533 \\text{ bits.}</span>\nThe marginal entropies are <span class=\"m\">H(X) = h(0.25) \\approx 0.811</span> bits and <span class=\"m\">H(Y) = h(0.35) \\approx 0.934</span> bits. Notice that <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) \\approx 1.533</span> is <strong>less</strong> than <span class=\"m\">H(X) + H(Y) \\approx 1.745</span>. The two variables overlap: part of the uncertainty about umbrellas is the same uncertainty as about the weather.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Joint entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The entropy of a pair of random variables treated as one: <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = -\\sum_{x, y} p(x, y)\\log_2 p(x, y)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"conditional-entropy-what-is-left-after-you-learn-x\" data-h=\"3\">Conditional entropy: what is left after you learn X</h3>\n<p>Now suppose someone tells you it is raining. Among rainy moments, the umbrella probabilities are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.20}{0.25} = 0.8</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.05}{0.25} = 0.2</span>, so your remaining uncertainty is <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X = \\text{rain}) = h(0.8) \\approx 0.722</span> bits. If instead it is dry, the probabilities are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.15}{0.75} = 0.2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.60}{0.75} = 0.8</span>, and again <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X = \\text{dry}) = h(0.2) \\approx 0.722</span> bits.</p>\n<p>The <strong>conditional entropy</strong> <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X)</span> is the average of these remaining uncertainties, weighted by how often each value of <span class=\"m\">X</span> happens:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(Y \\mid X) = \\sum_x p(x)\\,H(Y \\mid X = x) = 0.25(0.722) + 0.75(0.722) \\approx 0.722 \\text{ bits.}</span>\nBefore you looked at the weather, the umbrella was worth <span class=\"m\">H(Y) \\approx 0.934</span> bits of uncertainty. After, only 0.722 bits remain on average. Looking at the sky removed about <span class=\"m\">0.934 - 0.722 = 0.212</span> bits.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Conditional entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The average uncertainty left in <span class=\"m\">Y</span> once <span class=\"m\">X</span> is known: <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = \\sum_x p(x)H(Y \\mid X = x) = -\\sum_{x, y}p(x, y)\\log_2 p(y \\mid x)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-chain-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The chain rule</h3>\n<p>Here is a beautiful fact. Learning the pair <span class=\"m\">(X, Y)</span> is the same as learning <span class=\"m\">X</span> first and then learning <span class=\"m\">Y</span> given <span class=\"m\">X</span>. The uncertainties add in the same way:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X).</span>\nCheck it with the station: <span class=\"m\">0.811 + 0.722 = 1.533</span> bits, which is exactly the joint entropy we computed directly. It works the other way too: <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(Y) + H(X \\mid Y)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(X \\mid Y) = 1.533 - 0.934 = 0.599</span> bits. This rule comes straight from the product rule of probability, <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p(x)p(y \\mid x)</span>, because the logarithm turns that product into a sum.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chain rule for entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X)</span>: the uncertainty of a pair equals the uncertainty of the first variable plus the remaining uncertainty of the second.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"two-extreme-cases\" data-h=\"3\">Two extreme cases</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are <strong>independent</strong>, knowing <span class=\"m\">X</span> tells you nothing about <span class=\"m\">Y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = H(Y)</span> and the chain rule gives <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y)</span>. Two independent fair coins have <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = 2</span> bits.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is completely <strong>determined</strong> by <span class=\"m\">X</span> (for example <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is a copy of <span class=\"m\">X</span>), there is nothing left to learn, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X)</span>. A fair coin and its copy have <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = 1</span> bit, not 2.</p>\n<p>Every real situation sits between these two. In general, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le H(Y \\mid X) \\le H(Y)</span>: on average, <strong>conditioning never increases entropy</strong>. A single observation can make you more confused (if you learn something unexpected), but averaged over what you might observe, information never hurts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"information-gain-and-decision-trees\" data-h=\"3\">Information gain and decision trees</h3>\n<p>The drop in uncertainty, <span class=\"m\">H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X)</span>, is called the <strong>information gain</strong> of <span class=\"m\">X</span> about <span class=\"m\">Y</span>. For the station it was about 0.212 bits. This is how a decision tree chooses its questions. Suppose 16 emails are half spam, so the label entropy is 1 bit. Splitting on \"contains the word free\" gives one group of 6 emails (5 spam, 1 not) with entropy <span class=\"m\">h(\\frac56) \\approx 0.650</span> bits and another of 10 emails (3 spam, 7 not) with entropy <span class=\"m\">h(0.3) \\approx 0.881</span> bits. The weighted average is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6}{16}(0.650) + \\frac{10}{16}(0.881) \\approx 0.795</span> bits, so the information gain is about <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.795 = 0.205</span> bits. The tree tries every possible split and keeps the one with the biggest gain.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Information gain</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The reduction in entropy of a target <span class=\"m\">Y</span> from learning a feature <span class=\"m\">X</span>: <span class=\"m\">IG = H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X)</span>. Decision trees choose splits that maximize it. It equals the mutual information <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X)</span> is an <strong>average</strong> over <span class=\"m\">x</span>, not the entropy for one particular <span class=\"m\">x</span>. Always weight by <span class=\"m\">p(x)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X)</span> and <span class=\"m\">H(X \\mid Y)</span> are usually different numbers (0.722 versus 0.599 at the station).</li>\n<li>Joint entropy is not the sum of the marginal entropies unless the variables are independent.</li>\n<li>In decision trees, weight each child's entropy by the fraction of examples that go to that child, not by one half.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Joint entropy is the uncertainty of a pair, conditional entropy is the uncertainty left in one variable after learning the other, and the chain rule <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X)</span> ties them together. Conditioning reduces entropy on average, and the size of the reduction, the information gain, is what decision trees maximize. In the mutual information lesson the same gain gets its own name.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g07-joint\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Change the four joint probabilities and watch the entropy bars overlap.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Click <strong>independent</strong>. The orange <span class=\"m\">H(Y)</span> bar starts exactly where the purple <span class=\"m\">H(X)</span> bar ends, so there is no green overlap and <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y)</span>.\n2. Click <strong>Y is a copy of X</strong>. The two bars lie on top of each other, <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = 0</span>, and the joint bar is only 1 bit long.\n3. Click <strong>noisy copy</strong> and read <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X)</span>: some uncertainty remains, about 0.469 bits.\n4. Recreate the station table with sliders 0.20, 0.05, 0.15, 0.60 and check <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) \\approx 1.533</span>, <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) \\approx 0.722</span> and <span class=\"m\">H(X \\mid Y) \\approx 0.599</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the grey bar is always the purple bar plus the part of the orange bar that sticks out to the right. That stick-out length is <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X)</span>, which is the chain rule drawn as lengths. The overlap is the information gain.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) = -\\sum_{x, y}p(x, y)\\log_2 p(x, y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(Y \\mid X) = \\sum_x p(x)H(Y \\mid X = x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X) = H(Y) + H(X \\mid Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">0 \\le H(Y \\mid X) \\le H(Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">IG = H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chain rule for entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any random variables, <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X)</span>. More generally, <span class=\"m\">H(X_1, \\dots, X_n) = \\sum_{i} H(X_i \\mid X_1, \\dots, X_{i-1})</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the product rule, <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p(x)p(y \\mid x)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p(x, y) = -\\log_2 p(x) - \\log_2 p(y \\mid x)</span>. Take the expectation over <span class=\"m\">p(x, y)</span> on both sides. The left side becomes <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y)</span>. The first term on the right becomes <span class=\"m\">-\\sum_x p(x)\\log_2 p(x) = H(X)</span>, and the second becomes <span class=\"m\">-\\sum_{x, y}p(x, y)\\log_2 p(y \\mid x) = H(Y \\mid X)</span>. Applying the same argument repeatedly gives the version for <span class=\"m\">n</span> variables.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Conditioning reduces entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) \\le H(Y)</span>, with equality exactly when <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X) = \\sum_{x, y}p(x, y)\\log_2\\frac{p(x, y)}{p(x)p(y)}</span>, which is the KL divergence between <span class=\"m\">p(x, y)</span> and <span class=\"m\">p(x)p(y)</span>. KL divergence is never negative and is zero only when the two distributions are equal (proved in the KL lesson), that is, when <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p(x)p(y)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">x, y</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Station table: find H(Y), H(Y | X) and the information gain</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">p(\\text{umbrella}) = 0.35</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y) = h(0.35) \\approx 0.35(1.515) + 0.65(0.621) \\approx 0.934</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Given rain: <span class=\"m\">p(\\text{umbrella} \\mid \\text{rain}) = 0.8</span>, entropy <span class=\"m\">h(0.8) \\approx 0.722</span>. Given dry: <span class=\"m\">0.2</span>, entropy <span class=\"m\">h(0.2) \\approx 0.722</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = 0.25(0.722) + 0.75(0.722) = 0.722</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Information gain <span class=\"m\">= 0.934 - 0.722 \\approx 0.212</span> bits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decision tree split: 16 emails, 8 spam</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Parent entropy: <span class=\"m\">h(\\frac12) = 1</span> bit.</li>\n<li>Left child (contains \"free\"): 6 emails, 5 spam. <span class=\"m\">h(\\frac56) = \\frac56(0.263) + \\frac16(2.585) \\approx 0.650</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Right child: 10 emails, 3 spam. <span class=\"m\">h(0.3) \\approx 0.3(1.737) + 0.7(0.515) \\approx 0.881</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Weighted child entropy: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{6}{16}(0.650) + \\frac{10}{16}(0.881) \\approx 0.244 + 0.551 = 0.795</span> bits.</li>\n<li>Information gain <span class=\"m\">\\approx 1 - 0.795 = 0.205</span> bits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Use the chain rule: H(X) = 1.5 and H(Y | X) = 0.5</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X) = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2</span> bits.</li>\n<li>If also <span class=\"m\">H(Y) = 1.2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">H(X \\mid Y) = H(X, Y) - H(Y) = 0.8</span> bits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">X</span> is a fair bit. <span class=\"m\">Y</span> equals <span class=\"m\">X</span> with probability 0.9 and is flipped with probability 0.1. Find <span class=\"m\">H(Y)</span>, <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X)</span>, <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y)</span> and the information gain about <span class=\"m\">Y</span> from <span class=\"m\">X</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>By symmetry <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is also a fair bit, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y) = 1</span>. Whatever <span class=\"m\">X</span> is, <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is \"correct with probability 0.9\", so <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = h(0.1) \\approx 0.469</span> bits. The chain rule gives <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X) = 1 + 0.469 = 1.469</span> bits. The information gain is <span class=\"m\">1 - 0.469 = 0.531</span> bits.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Decision trees and random forests</strong> (ID3, C4.5, and scikit-learn with <code>criterion=\"entropy\"</code>) split on the feature with the highest information gain. Gradient-boosted trees use similar impurity reductions.</li>\n<li><strong>Language models</strong> use the chain rule directly: the entropy of a sentence is the sum of the conditional entropies of each token given the previous ones, which is why models are trained to predict one next token at a time.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compressors that use context (like the PPM family and modern neural compressors) code each symbol using <span class=\"m\">p(\\text{next} \\mid \\text{context})</span>. Their cost per symbol is the conditional entropy <span class=\"m\">H(X_t \\mid X_{&lt;t})</span>, which is much smaller than <span class=\"m\">H(X_t)</span> for text.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A doctor ordering tests is maximizing information gain: the best next test is the one whose result most reduces uncertainty about the diagnosis, averaged over the possible results. A test whose result you can already predict has nearly zero gain, however expensive it is.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">X and Y are independent fair coins. What is H(X, Y) in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Independent, so <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y) = 1 + 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Y is an exact copy of a fair coin X. What is H(X, Y)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = 0</span> because <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is determined, so <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">H(X) = 1.5 bits and H(Y | X) = 0.5 bits. Find H(X, Y).</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Chain rule: <span class=\"m\">1.5 + 0.5 = 2</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can H(Y | X) be larger than H(Y)?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, often</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, conditioning never increases entropy on average</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Only when X and Y are independent</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) \\le H(Y)</span> always, with equality exactly when they are independent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A parent node has entropy 1 bit; after a split the weighted child entropy is 0.6 bits. What is the information gain?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.4|2/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">IG = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If Y is a deterministic function of X, then H(Y | X) =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Once you know <span class=\"m\">X</span> you know <span class=\"m\">Y</span> exactly, so no uncertainty remains.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "source-coding": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-compression-is-an-information-question\" data-h=\"3\">Why compression is an information question</h3>\n<p>Every file on your computer is a long string of symbols, and storing or sending it costs bits. If some symbols appear much more often than others, you can save space by giving them shorter codes. This is the idea behind ZIP files, PNG images, the MP3 and JPEG formats, and even Morse code. Information theory tells us exactly how much saving is possible: on average, no lossless code can use fewer bits per symbol than the entropy of the source. This lesson builds the codes that come close to that limit.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine you send weather reports from a mountain station using only 0s and 1s. There are four reports: Sunny (half of all days), Cloudy (a quarter), Rain (an eighth) and Snow (an eighth). The obvious plan is a <strong>fixed-length code</strong> with 2 bits each: Sunny <code>00</code>, Cloudy <code>01</code>, Rain <code>10</code>, Snow <code>11</code>. That costs exactly 2 bits per report.</p>\n<p>Now try a <strong>variable-length code</strong>: Sunny <code>0</code>, Cloudy <code>10</code>, Rain <code>110</code>, Snow <code>111</code>. Sunny reports cost only 1 bit, and they are the most common. The average cost is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\tfrac12(1) + \\tfrac14(2) + \\tfrac18(3) + \\tfrac18(3) = 1.75 \\text{ bits per report,}</span>\nwhich saves 12.5%. A year of 1000 reports needs about 1750 bits instead of 2000. And 1.75 bits is exactly the entropy of this distribution, so no code can do better.</p>\n<h3 id=\"decoding-without-commas-prefix-codes\" data-h=\"3\">Decoding without commas: prefix codes</h3>\n<p>Variable-length codes have a danger. Suppose we used A = <code>0</code>, B = <code>01</code>, C = <code>10</code>. The message <code>010</code> could mean A then C, or B then A. We can't tell where one codeword ends and the next begins. The fix is to use a <strong>prefix code</strong> (also called prefix-free or instantaneous): no codeword is the beginning of another. In our weather code, <code>0</code> is not the start of <code>10</code>, <code>110</code> or <code>111</code>, and <code>10</code> is not the start of <code>110</code> or <code>111</code>. So when the decoder reads <code>0</code>, it knows instantly that the report was Sunny. Reading <code>110100</code> gives Rain, Cloudy, Sunny with no ambiguity.</p>\n<p>A prefix code is the same thing as a binary tree. Start at the root; a <code>0</code> means \"go left\" and a <code>1</code> means \"go right\". Each codeword is the path to a <strong>leaf</strong>. Because codewords are leaves, no codeword can lie on the path to another, which is exactly the prefix property.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prefix code</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A code in which no codeword is a prefix (beginning) of any other codeword. It can be decoded symbol by symbol with no separators, and its codewords are the leaves of a binary tree.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-short-can-codewords-be-the-kraft-inequality\" data-h=\"3\">How short can codewords be? The Kraft inequality</h3>\n<p>You can't make every codeword short. With only 1-bit codewords there are just two: <code>0</code> and <code>1</code>. A rule called the <strong>Kraft inequality</strong> says exactly which lengths are possible. A prefix code with lengths <span class=\"m\">\\ell_1, \\ell_2, \\dots, \\ell_n</span> exists if and only if\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_{i=1}^n 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1.</span>\nThink of <span class=\"m\">2^{-\\ell}</span> as the \"budget\" a codeword uses: a 1-bit codeword uses half of all possible binary strings (all strings starting with <code>0</code>), a 2-bit codeword uses a quarter, and so on. The total budget is 1. Our weather code uses <span class=\"m\">\\frac12 + \\frac14 + \\frac18 + \\frac18 = 1</span>, the whole budget. Lengths <span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 2)</span> would need <span class=\"m\">\\frac12 + \\frac12 + \\frac14 = 1.25 &gt; 1</span>, so no prefix code has those lengths.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Kraft inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lengths <span class=\"m\">\\ell_1, \\dots, \\ell_n</span> can be the codeword lengths of a binary prefix code exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-ideal-length-is-the-surprise\" data-h=\"3\">The ideal length is the surprise</h3>\n<p>Which lengths minimize the average <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i p_i\\ell_i</span> subject to the Kraft budget? The answer is <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i = -\\log_2 p_i</span>, the surprise of the symbol. Common symbols get short codes and rare symbols long ones, in exactly the amounts that information theory predicts. Plugging these lengths in gives an average of <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i p_i(-\\log_2 p_i) = H</span>, the entropy. When the probabilities are powers of <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>, as in the weather example, these ideal lengths are whole numbers and we hit the entropy exactly. Otherwise we must round up, using <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i = \\lceil -\\log_2 p_i\\rceil</span>, which costs less than 1 extra bit per symbol.</p>\n<h3 id=\"huffman-s-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">Huffman's algorithm</h3>\n<p>In 1952 David Huffman, then a student, found a simple procedure that builds the best possible prefix code for any list of probabilities:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Write each symbol as a tiny tree with its probability.</li>\n<li>Take the two trees with the <strong>smallest</strong> probabilities and join them under a new node whose probability is their sum.</li>\n<li>Repeat until only one tree is left.</li>\n<li>Label every left branch <code>0</code> and every right branch <code>1</code>; each symbol's code is its path from the root.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Try it on probabilities A 0.4, B 0.2, C 0.2, D 0.1, E 0.1. Join D and E into a node of 0.2. Now the smallest are three nodes of 0.2; join two of them into 0.4. Then join the remaining 0.2 with a 0.4 to make 0.6, and finally 0.6 with 0.4 to make 1. One valid outcome gives lengths A 1, B 2, C 3, D 4, E 4, with average length <span class=\"m\">0.4 + 0.4 + 0.6 + 0.4 + 0.4 = 2.2</span> bits. The entropy is about 2.122 bits, so Huffman is within 0.08 bits of the limit, and far better than the 3 bits a fixed-length code needs for 5 symbols.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Huffman code</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The optimal prefix code for known symbol probabilities, built by repeatedly merging the two least likely symbols or subtrees. Its average length <span class=\"m\">\\bar L</span> satisfies <span class=\"m\">H \\le \\bar L &lt; H + 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"getting-even-closer-coding-blocks\" data-h=\"3\">Getting even closer: coding blocks</h3>\n<p>The \"less than 1 extra bit\" can hurt when a source is very predictable. A coin with <span class=\"m\">p = 0.9</span> has entropy 0.469 bits, but any code must use at least 1 bit per single flip. The fix is to code <strong>blocks</strong> of symbols together: pairs of flips have four outcomes, and coding them costs less than 1 extra bit per pair, which is less than half a bit per flip. With longer blocks the overhead per symbol shrinks toward zero, and the average approaches <span class=\"m\">H</span>. Modern <strong>arithmetic coding</strong> achieves this effect smoothly and gets within a tiny fraction of a bit of the entropy for the whole message.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Huffman needs the <strong>two smallest</strong> at every step, including merged nodes. Don't merge in the original order.</li>\n<li>Ties can give different trees with different individual lengths, but every Huffman tree has the same average length.</li>\n<li>A code that is not prefix-free can be impossible to decode even if every codeword is distinct.</li>\n<li>Compression works only on average. Some individual messages always get longer, and already-random data can't be compressed at all.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A prefix code can be decoded without separators and corresponds to a binary tree. The Kraft inequality <span class=\"m\">\\sum 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1</span> limits how short the codewords can be. The ideal length of a symbol is its surprise <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p</span>, which makes the average length equal to the entropy. Huffman's algorithm gives the best prefix code, within 1 bit of <span class=\"m\">H</span>, and coding long blocks gets as close to <span class=\"m\">H</span> as you like. That is Shannon's source coding theorem.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g07-huffman\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Move the weight sliders and watch Huffman rebuild the tree.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Click <strong>powers of 1/2</strong>. The average length equals the entropy exactly (100% efficiency), because every ideal length <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p</span> is a whole number.\n2. Click <strong>skewed</strong> and check the article: average length 2.2 bits, entropy about 2.122 bits.\n3. Click <strong>uniform</strong>. With 5 equal symbols the codes have lengths 2 or 3, average 2.4 bits, against an entropy of <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 5 \\approx 2.32</span>.\n4. Click <strong>one dominant</strong>. Entropy falls to about 0.65 bits but the average stays above 1 bit, because every symbol needs at least 1 bit.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the surprise column and the length column track each other. Huffman lengths are whole-number roundings of the surprise, and the average always lands in the window <span class=\"m\">H \\le \\bar L &lt; H + 1</span>. Case 4 shows why block coding is needed for very predictable sources.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\bar L = \\sum_i p_i\\ell_i</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_i 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1 \\quad\\text{(Kraft)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\ell_i^{\\ast} = -\\log_2 p_i,\\qquad \\ell_i = \\lceil -\\log_2 p_i\\rceil</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X) \\le \\bar L_{\\text{Huffman}} &lt; H(X) + 1</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Shannon's source coding theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any prefix code, the average length satisfies <span class=\"m\">\\bar L \\ge H(X)</span>. There is a prefix code with <span class=\"m\">\\bar L &lt; H(X) + 1</span>, and by coding blocks of <span class=\"m\">k</span> symbols the average per symbol can be made less than <span class=\"m\">H(X) + \\frac1k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lower bound: let <span class=\"m\">Z = \\sum_i 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1</span> and define the distribution <span class=\"m\">q_i = 2^{-\\ell_i}/Z</span>. Then\n<span class=\"mb\">\\bar L - H = \\sum_i p_i\\ell_i + \\sum_i p_i\\log_2 p_i = \\sum_i p_i\\log_2\\frac{p_i}{2^{-\\ell_i}} = \\sum_i p_i\\log_2\\frac{p_i}{q_i} - \\log_2 Z.</span>\nThe first sum is a KL divergence, which is <span class=\"m\">\\ge 0</span>, and <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 Z \\ge 0</span> because <span class=\"m\">Z \\le 1</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\bar L \\ge H</span>. Upper bound: choose <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i = \\lceil -\\log_2 p_i\\rceil</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">2^{-\\ell_i} \\le p_i</span>, so the Kraft sum is at most <span class=\"m\">\\sum p_i = 1</span> and the code exists. Since <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i &lt; -\\log_2 p_i + 1</span>, we get <span class=\"m\">\\bar L &lt; H + 1</span>. Applying this to blocks of <span class=\"m\">k</span> symbols, whose entropy is <span class=\"m\">kH</span>, gives less than <span class=\"m\">kH + 1</span> bits per block, or <span class=\"m\">H + \\frac1k</span> per symbol.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Kraft inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A binary prefix code with lengths <span class=\"m\">\\ell_1, \\dots, \\ell_n</span> exists if and only if <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\ell_{\\max}</span> be the longest length. A codeword of length <span class=\"m\">\\ell</span> is a prefix of exactly <span class=\"m\">2^{\\ell_{\\max} - \\ell}</span> of the <span class=\"m\">2^{\\ell_{\\max}}</span> strings of length <span class=\"m\">\\ell_{\\max}</span>. In a prefix code these sets don't overlap, so <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i 2^{\\ell_{\\max} - \\ell_i} \\le 2^{\\ell_{\\max}}</span>; divide by <span class=\"m\">2^{\\ell_{\\max}}</span>. Conversely, if the inequality holds, assign codewords in order of increasing length, each time taking the leftmost unused node at the right depth; the budget guarantees you never run out.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Build a Huffman code for A 0.4, B 0.2, C 0.2, D 0.1, E 0.1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Merge the two smallest, D and E: new node 0.2. Remaining: 0.4, 0.2, 0.2, 0.2.</li>\n<li>Merge two of the 0.2s (say C and DE): node 0.4. Remaining: 0.4, 0.4, 0.2.</li>\n<li>Merge 0.2 (B) with a 0.4 (C, DE): node 0.6. Remaining: 0.6, 0.4.</li>\n<li>Merge the last two: root 1.0. Reading paths gives A <code>0</code>, B <code>10</code>, C <code>110</code>, D <code>1110</code>, E <code>1111</code>.</li>\n<li>Average length <span class=\"m\">= 0.4(1) + 0.2(2) + 0.2(3) + 0.1(4) + 0.1(4) = 2.2</span> bits. Entropy <span class=\"m\">\\approx 2.122</span> bits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Which length lists can a prefix code have: (1, 2, 3, 3) and (1, 1, 2)?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3, 3)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12 + \\frac14 + \\frac18 + \\frac18 = 1 \\le 1</span>. Possible, for example <code>0</code>, <code>10</code>, <code>110</code>, <code>111</code>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 2)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12 + \\frac12 + \\frac14 = 1.25 &gt; 1</span>. Impossible: the two 1-bit codewords use up every string.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How big is a 1000-symbol file from (1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/8)?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Fixed-length code: 2 bits each, so 2000 bits.</li>\n<li>Huffman code <code>0</code>, <code>10</code>, <code>110</code>, <code>111</code>: 1.75 bits each on average, so about 1750 bits.</li>\n<li>Entropy is 1.75 bits, so 1750 bits is the best any lossless code can do on average.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A source emits A, B, C with probabilities 0.5, 0.3, 0.2. Build a Huffman code, find its average length, and compare it with the entropy.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Merge the two smallest, C (0.2) and B (0.3), into 0.5, then merge with A. Codes: A <code>0</code>, B <code>10</code>, C <code>11</code>. Average length <span class=\"m\">= 0.5(1) + 0.3(2) + 0.2(2) = 1.5</span> bits. Entropy <span class=\"m\">= 0.5(1) + 0.3\\log_2\\frac1{0.3} + 0.2\\log_2\\frac1{0.2} \\approx 0.5 + 0.521 + 0.464 = 1.485</span> bits. Efficiency <span class=\"m\">\\approx 1.485/1.5 = 99\\%</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Prediction is compression.</strong> A model that assigns probability <span class=\"m\">q</span> to the next symbol can be turned, via arithmetic coding, into a compressor using about <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 q</span> bits for it. Lower cross-entropy loss literally means better compression; large language models compress text better than ZIP.</li>\n<li><strong>Tokenizers</strong> such as byte-pair encoding (BPE) are compression algorithms: they repeatedly merge the most frequent pair of symbols, much like Huffman merges the least frequent.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum description length:</strong> the best model is the one that gives the shortest total description of model plus data, a principled guard against overfitting.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>ZIP and gzip use DEFLATE, which finds repeated substrings (LZ77) and then Huffman-codes the result. JPEG and MP3 Huffman-code their quantized coefficients, and PNG uses DEFLATE. Video codecs use arithmetic coding (CABAC) to get closer to the entropy.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Morse code is a hand-made variable-length code: E, the most common English letter, is a single dot, while rare letters like Q get four symbols. Samuel Morse reportedly estimated letter frequencies by counting the type in a printer's case. (Morse is not prefix-free on its own; it relies on pauses between letters as separators.)</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A fixed-length binary code for 8 equally likely symbols needs how many bits per symbol?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span> codewords of length 3, which also equals the entropy <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 8</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is {0, 01, 11} a prefix code?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><code>0</code> is the beginning of <code>01</code>, so a decoder reading <code>0</code> can't tell whether the symbol is finished.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the Kraft sum for codeword lengths (2, 2, 2, 2)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4 \\times 2^{-2} = 1</span>. The four 2-bit codewords use the whole budget.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Code A = 0, B = 10, C = 11 with probabilities 0.5, 0.25, 0.25. Average length in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.5(1) + 0.25(2) + 0.25(2) = 1.5</span>, which equals the entropy, so the code is optimal.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can a Huffman code have average length below the entropy?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, for skewed sources</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, H is a lower bound for every lossless prefix code</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Only for uniform sources</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Shannon's source coding theorem: <span class=\"m\">\\bar L \\ge H</span> for every prefix code.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A source has entropy 2.3 bits. The Huffman average length must lie in which range?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">From 2.3 up to (but not including) 3.3 bits</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Exactly 2.3 bits</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Below 2.3 bits</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">H \\le \\bar L &lt; H + 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "cross-entropy": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-a-second-distribution\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need a second distribution</h3>\n<p>Entropy measures the uncertainty of the true distribution <span class=\"m\">p</span>. But in practice we never know <span class=\"m\">p</span> exactly. A weather app, a spam filter or a neural network only has a <strong>model</strong> <span class=\"m\">q</span>, its best guess at the probabilities. The natural question is: how good is that guess? If we use <span class=\"m\">q</span> to anticipate outcomes that actually come from <span class=\"m\">p</span>, how surprised will we be on average? That average is the <strong>cross-entropy</strong>, and it is the loss function behind almost every classifier and language model in use today.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a tourist arrives in a city and assumes the weather is like at home: sunny, cloudy and rainy each one third of the time. In reality this city is sunny half the time and cloudy or rainy a quarter each, so <span class=\"m\">p = (\\frac12, \\frac14, \\frac14)</span> and the tourist's belief is <span class=\"m\">q = (\\frac13, \\frac13, \\frac13)</span>. Every morning the tourist feels a surprise of <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 q(\\text{weather}) = \\log_2 3 \\approx 1.585</span> bits, whatever the weather. A local who knows the true <span class=\"m\">p</span> feels on average only <span class=\"m\">H(p) = 1.5</span> bits. The tourist pays an extra <span class=\"m\">0.085</span> bits per day for holding the wrong belief.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>The <strong>cross-entropy</strong> of <span class=\"m\">q</span> relative to <span class=\"m\">p</span> is the expected surprise under <span class=\"m\">q</span> when outcomes are drawn from <span class=\"m\">p</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log q(x).</span>\nThe weights <span class=\"m\">p(x)</span> come from reality (how often each outcome really happens), and the surprises <span class=\"m\">-\\log q(x)</span> come from the model. Compare entropy, <span class=\"m\">H(p) = -\\sum p\\log p</span>, where both come from <span class=\"m\">p</span>. When the model is perfect, <span class=\"m\">q = p</span> and the cross-entropy equals the entropy.</p>\n<p>In coding language, <span class=\"m\">H(p, q)</span> is the average length of a code designed for <span class=\"m\">q</span> (lengths <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 q(x)</span>) but used on data from <span class=\"m\">p</span>. A code built for the wrong distribution wastes bits.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cross-entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">H(p, q) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log q(x)</span>: the average surprise, or code length, when outcomes follow <span class=\"m\">p</span> but probabilities are taken from the model <span class=\"m\">q</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"cross-entropy-is-never-smaller-than-entropy\" data-h=\"3\">Cross-entropy is never smaller than entropy</h3>\n<p>A key fact, called <strong>Gibbs' inequality</strong>, is that\n<span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) \\ge H(p),</span>\nwith equality only when <span class=\"m\">q = p</span>. You can never do better on average than knowing the truth. The gap <span class=\"m\">H(p, q) - H(p)</span> is the KL divergence of the next lesson, the exact price of a wrong belief. For the tourist, the gap is <span class=\"m\">1.585 - 1.5 = 0.085</span> bits.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cross-entropy-as-a-classification-loss\" data-h=\"3\">Cross-entropy as a classification loss</h3>\n<p>Now the big application. A classifier sees an image and outputs probabilities over classes, for example <span class=\"m\">q = (0.7, 0.2, 0.1)</span> for (cat, dog, bird). The true label is \"cat\". We write the truth as a <strong>one-hot</strong> distribution <span class=\"m\">p = (1, 0, 0)</span>: all probability on the correct class. Then every term of the cross-entropy except the true class vanishes:\n<span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) = -1\\cdot\\log q(\\text{cat}) = -\\ln 0.7 \\approx 0.357 \\text{ nats.}</span>\nSo the cross-entropy loss is simply <strong>minus the log of the probability the model gave to the right answer</strong>. If the model had given the cat only 0.1, the loss would be <span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.1 \\approx 2.303</span> nats. If it had given 0.99, the loss would be about 0.010. The loss rewards confidence when you are right and punishes confidence when you are wrong; as <span class=\"m\">q(\\text{true}) \\to 0</span> the loss goes to infinity.</p>\n<p>Averaged over a dataset of <span class=\"m\">N</span> examples, the loss is <span class=\"m\">-\\frac1N\\sum_i \\log q(y_i \\mid x_i)</span>, the <strong>negative log-likelihood</strong> per example. So minimizing cross-entropy is the same as <strong>maximum likelihood estimation</strong>: choose model parameters that make the observed labels as probable as possible.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cross-entropy loss</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a classifier with predicted probabilities <span class=\"m\">q</span> and true class <span class=\"m\">y</span>: <span class=\"m\">L = -\\log q_y</span>. Averaged over the data, it is the negative log-likelihood.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-binary-case\" data-h=\"3\">The binary case</h3>\n<p>For yes/no problems the model outputs one probability <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = P(\\text{class } 1)</span>, usually from a sigmoid. With true label <span class=\"m\">y \\in \\{0, 1\\}</span> the cross-entropy is the <strong>binary cross-entropy</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">L = -\\big[y\\log\\hat y + (1 - y)\\log(1 - \\hat y)\\big].</span>\nOnly one of the two terms is \"switched on\": if <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span> the loss is <span class=\"m\">-\\log\\hat y</span>, and if <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span> it is <span class=\"m\">-\\log(1 - \\hat y)</span>. A model that always says <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 0.5</span> pays <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span> nats per example, which is why 0.693 is the loss you see at the start of training a binary classifier.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-softmax-and-cross-entropy-go-together\" data-h=\"3\">Why softmax and cross-entropy go together</h3>\n<p>Neural networks produce raw scores <span class=\"m\">z_i</span> (logits), which softmax turns into probabilities <span class=\"m\">q_i = e^{z_i}/\\sum_j e^{z_j}</span>. When you combine softmax with cross-entropy, the gradient of the loss with respect to each logit is astonishingly simple:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z_i} = q_i - p_i.</span>\nThe gradient is \"prediction minus truth\". It is large when the model is confidently wrong and shrinks to zero as the prediction approaches the target. This clean gradient is a big reason cross-entropy trains much better than, say, squared error on probabilities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"perplexity\" data-h=\"3\">Perplexity</h3>\n<p>Language models report <strong>perplexity</strong>, the exponential of the cross-entropy: <span class=\"m\">2^{H}</span> if <span class=\"m\">H</span> is in bits, or <span class=\"m\">e^{H}</span> if in nats. A perplexity of 8 means the model is, on average, as uncertain as if it were choosing uniformly among 8 tokens. Lower is better, and a perfect model would reach the true entropy of the language.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Order matters: <span class=\"m\">H(p, q)</span> uses <span class=\"m\">p</span> for the weights and <span class=\"m\">q</span> inside the log. <span class=\"m\">H(q, p)</span> is a different number.</li>\n<li>Never feed a probability of exactly 0 into the log. Frameworks compute cross-entropy from logits (for example <code>log_softmax</code>) to stay numerically safe.</li>\n<li>Cross-entropy loss is not \"accuracy\". A model can be right on every example and still have high loss if it is barely confident.</li>\n<li>Losses printed by PyTorch or TensorFlow are in nats, not bits.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Cross-entropy <span class=\"m\">H(p, q) = -\\sum p\\log q</span> is the average surprise when reality is <span class=\"m\">p</span> and your model is <span class=\"m\">q</span>. It is always at least the entropy, with equality only for a perfect model. With one-hot labels it reduces to <span class=\"m\">-\\log q(\\text{true class})</span>, the standard classification loss, which is the same as maximum likelihood. Its gradient with softmax is simply prediction minus truth.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"entropy\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Set p, then move the q bars and watch H(p, q) rise above H(p).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Make the q sliders match the p sliders exactly. The cross-entropy equals the entropy and KL reads 0.\n2. Set p to (0.5, 0.25, 0.125, 0.125) and q uniform: <span class=\"m\">H(p) = 1.75</span> and <span class=\"m\">H(p, q) = 2</span> bits.\n3. Make p1 large, then shrink q1 toward 0.01. Watch the cross-entropy shoot up: the model is confidently wrong about the most common outcome.\n4. Imitate a one-hot label: put p1 at 1 and the other p sliders at 0.01, then raise q1 and see the loss fall.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> no setting of q ever makes <span class=\"m\">H(p, q)</span> smaller than <span class=\"m\">H(p)</span>, which is Gibbs' inequality. Step 4 shows the classification case: the loss depends almost only on q of the true class.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"sigmoid\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Move the logits and the temperature; the softmax bars are the q a classifier would output.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Treat class 1 (pink) as the true class. Raise <span class=\"m\">z_1</span> and watch its softmax probability rise; the loss <span class=\"m\">-\\ln q_1</span> falls.\n2. Lower the temperature T toward 0.1: probabilities become nearly one-hot, so a right answer gets near-zero loss but a wrong one gets a huge loss.\n3. Raise T toward 5: everything flattens toward uniform and the loss approaches <span class=\"m\">\\ln 4 \\approx 1.386</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> logits only matter through their differences, and confidence is a double-edged sword under cross-entropy. That is why techniques like label smoothing and temperature scaling exist.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log q(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) \\ge H(p),\\quad H(p, q) = H(p) + D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L_{\\text{CE}} = -\\log q_y,\\qquad L_{\\text{BCE}} = -[y\\log\\hat y + (1-y)\\log(1-\\hat y)]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z_i} = q_i - p_i \\quad\\text{(softmax + cross-entropy)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{perplexity} = 2^{H_{\\text{bits}}} = e^{H_{\\text{nats}}}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gibbs' inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For distributions <span class=\"m\">p</span> and <span class=\"m\">q</span> on the same outcomes, <span class=\"m\">H(p, q) \\ge H(p)</span>, with equality if and only if <span class=\"m\">q = p</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Use natural logs and the inequality <span class=\"m\">\\ln t \\le t - 1</span> (equality only at <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>). Summing over outcomes with <span class=\"m\">p(x) &gt; 0</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">H(p) - H(p, q) = \\sum_x p(x)\\ln\\frac{q(x)}{p(x)} \\le \\sum_x p(x)\\Big(\\frac{q(x)}{p(x)} - 1\\Big) = \\sum_x q(x) - \\sum_x p(x) \\le 1 - 1 = 0.</span>\nEquality needs <span class=\"m\">q(x)/p(x) = 1</span> for every <span class=\"m\">x</span>, so <span class=\"m\">q = p</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient of softmax cross-entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">q = \\text{softmax}(z)</span> and <span class=\"m\">L = -\\sum_i p_i\\ln q_i</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i p_i = 1</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z_k} = q_k - p_k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\ln q_i = z_i - \\ln\\sum_j e^{z_j}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L = -\\sum_i p_i z_i + \\ln\\sum_j e^{z_j}</span> (using <span class=\"m\">\\sum p_i = 1</span>). Differentiating, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z_k} = -p_k + \\frac{e^{z_k}}{\\sum_j e^{z_j}} = q_k - p_k</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Predicted (0.7, 0.2, 0.1), true class 1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>One-hot truth <span class=\"m\">p = (1, 0, 0)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L = -\\ln q_1 = -\\ln 0.7 \\approx 0.357</span> nats.</li>\n<li>If the model had said 0.1 for the true class: <span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.1 \\approx 2.303</span> nats.</li>\n<li>Confident mistakes are punished heavily: the loss grows without bound as <span class=\"m\">q_1 \\to 0</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Binary cross-entropy for labels (1, 0, 1) and predictions (0.9, 0.2, 0.6)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Example 1, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.9 \\approx 0.105</span>.</li>\n<li>Example 2, <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">-\\ln(1 - 0.2) = -\\ln 0.8 \\approx 0.223</span>.</li>\n<li>Example 3, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.6 \\approx 0.511</span>.</li>\n<li>Mean loss <span class=\"m\">= (0.105 + 0.223 + 0.511)/3 \\approx 0.280</span> nats.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Softmax of logits (2, 1, 0) with true class 1: loss and gradient</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">e^2 \\approx 7.389</span>, <span class=\"m\">e^1 \\approx 2.718</span>, <span class=\"m\">e^0 = 1</span>; total <span class=\"m\">\\approx 11.107</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q \\approx (0.665, 0.245, 0.090)</span>.</li>\n<li>Loss <span class=\"m\">= -\\ln 0.665 \\approx 0.408</span> nats.</li>\n<li>Gradient <span class=\"m\">q - p \\approx (0.665 - 1, 0.245, 0.090) = (-0.335, 0.245, 0.090)</span>. Gradient descent raises <span class=\"m\">z_1</span> and lowers the others.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language model has an average cross-entropy of 3 bits per token on test text. (a) What is its perplexity? (b) After training longer the loss drops to 2.5 bits per token. What is the new perplexity, and what does it mean?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Perplexity <span class=\"m\">= 2^3 = 8</span>: on average the model is as unsure as a uniform choice among 8 tokens. (b) <span class=\"m\">2^{2.5} = 4\\sqrt2 \\approx 5.66</span>. The model now behaves as if choosing among about 5.7 equally likely tokens. Half a bit less per token shrinks the effective number of choices by a factor of <span class=\"m\">2^{0.5} \\approx 1.41</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Classification:</strong> <code>nn.CrossEntropyLoss</code> in PyTorch takes logits and true class indices and computes <span class=\"m\">-\\log\\text{softmax}(z)_y</span> averaged over the batch.</li>\n<li><strong>Language models</strong> are trained to minimize next-token cross-entropy, and evaluated by perplexity.</li>\n<li><strong>Label smoothing</strong> replaces the one-hot <span class=\"m\">p</span> with, say, 0.9 on the true class and the remaining 0.1 spread over the others, so the model is never pushed toward infinite confidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge distillation</strong> trains a small student on the soft probabilities of a large teacher, using cross-entropy with a non-one-hot <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Cross-entropy is the cost of compressing data with the wrong model. If a compressor's model is <span class=\"m\">q</span> but the data follows <span class=\"m\">p</span>, it spends <span class=\"m\">H(p, q)</span> bits per symbol instead of the optimal <span class=\"m\">H(p)</span>. Better predictive models therefore make better compressors.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Weather services and forecasting competitions score probabilistic forecasts with the <strong>log score</strong>, which is cross-entropy: you are charged <span class=\"m\">-\\log q</span> of what actually happened. A forecaster who says \"0% chance of rain\" on a rainy day gets an infinite penalty, which encourages honest, calibrated probabilities.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Cross-entropy loss (natural log) when the true class gets probability 1?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\ln 1 = 0</span>: a perfect, confident prediction has zero loss.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A 4-class model outputs the uniform distribution. What is its cross-entropy loss in nats (3 decimals)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.386|ln 4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\ln\\frac14 = \\ln 4 \\approx 1.386</span> for any true class.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Binary cross-entropy for y = 1 and prediction 0.5, in nats (3 decimals)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.693|ln 2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.5 = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which prediction for the true class gives the largest loss?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0.9</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">0.5</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">0.01</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.01 \\approx 4.6</span>, far more than <span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.5 \\approx 0.69</span> or <span class=\"m\">-\\ln 0.9 \\approx 0.11</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Average cross-entropy is 2 bits per token. What is the perplexity?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^2 = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which is always true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">H(p, q) \\ge H(p)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">H(p, q) = H(q, p)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">H(p, q) \\le H(p)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Gibbs' inequality. Cross-entropy is not symmetric in general.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "kl": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"measuring-how-wrong-a-belief-is\" data-h=\"3\">Measuring how wrong a belief is</h3>\n<p>Cross-entropy told us the total surprise of using a model <span class=\"m\">q</span> when reality is <span class=\"m\">p</span>. Part of that surprise is unavoidable: even a perfect model feels the entropy <span class=\"m\">H(p)</span>. What we really want to measure is the <strong>extra</strong> surprise caused by the model being wrong. That extra is the <strong>Kullback–Leibler divergence</strong>, or KL divergence, named after Solomon Kullback and Richard Leibler who introduced it in 1951. It is the most common way to measure how different two probability distributions are, and it appears in variational autoencoders, reinforcement learning from human feedback, knowledge distillation and Bayesian inference.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine a coin that is perfectly fair, <span class=\"m\">p = (\\frac12, \\frac12)</span>, but a gambler believes it lands heads 90% of the time, <span class=\"m\">q = (0.9, 0.1)</span>. Each time tails comes up, the gambler is very surprised: <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.1 \\approx 3.32</span> bits instead of the 1 bit a correct believer would feel. Each time heads comes up, the gambler is a little less surprised than necessary: <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 0.9 \\approx 0.15</span> bits instead of 1. On average the gambler feels <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(0.152) + \\frac12(3.322) \\approx 1.737</span> bits of surprise per flip, compared with <span class=\"m\">H(p) = 1</span> bit. The difference, about <span class=\"m\">0.737</span> bits per flip, is the KL divergence. It is the price of the wrong belief.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p><span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) = \\sum_x p(x)\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} = H(p, q) - H(p).</span>\nRead the double bar as \"from <span class=\"m\">q</span> to <span class=\"m\">p</span>\" or \"of <span class=\"m\">q</span> relative to <span class=\"m\">p</span>\": the expectation is always taken under <span class=\"m\">p</span>, the distribution we treat as the truth. Each term compares the two probabilities through the log-ratio <span class=\"m\">\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</span>, which is positive where the model underestimates and negative where it overestimates. The weighted sum always comes out non-negative.</p>\n<p>Let's verify the gambler: <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) = \\frac12\\log_2\\frac{0.5}{0.9} + \\frac12\\log_2\\frac{0.5}{0.1} \\approx \\frac12(-0.848) + \\frac12(2.322) = 0.737</span> bits. Same answer as the cross-entropy route.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">KL divergence</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) = \\sum_x p(x)\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</span>: the expected extra surprise (or extra code length) from using <span class=\"m\">q</span> when the truth is <span class=\"m\">p</span>. Also called relative entropy.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"kl-is-never-negative\" data-h=\"3\">KL is never negative</h3>\n<p>By Gibbs' inequality from the previous lesson, <span class=\"m\">H(p, q) \\ge H(p)</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) \\ge 0,</span>\nwith equality exactly when <span class=\"m\">p = q</span>. This makes KL behave like a measure of distance: zero when the distributions agree, positive otherwise, and larger when they differ more.</p>\n<h3 id=\"kl-is-not-symmetric\" data-h=\"3\">KL is not symmetric</h3>\n<p>But KL is <strong>not</strong> a true distance, because swapping the arguments usually changes the answer. Compute the gambler's case the other way round, treating <span class=\"m\">q = (0.9, 0.1)</span> as the truth:\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(q\\,\\|\\,p) = 0.9\\log_2\\frac{0.9}{0.5} + 0.1\\log_2\\frac{0.1}{0.5} \\approx 0.9(0.848) + 0.1(-2.322) \\approx 0.531 \\text{ bits.}</span>\nSo <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q) \\approx 0.737</span> but <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(q\\|p) \\approx 0.531</span>. Which direction you use is a modelling choice with real consequences, as we'll see.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-kl-becomes-infinite\" data-h=\"3\">When KL becomes infinite</h3>\n<p>Look at the term <span class=\"m\">p(x)\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</span>. If the model says <span class=\"m\">q(x) = 0</span> for an outcome that really can happen (<span class=\"m\">p(x) &gt; 0</span>), the term is infinite. Saying \"impossible\" about something possible is the worst mistake a model can make, and KL punishes it without limit. The reverse is harmless: if <span class=\"m\">p(x) = 0</span>, the term is <span class=\"m\">0</span> no matter what <span class=\"m\">q(x)</span> is. This is why models should keep a little probability on everything plausible.</p>\n<h3 id=\"forward-versus-reverse-kl\" data-h=\"3\">Forward versus reverse KL</h3>\n<p>Suppose the truth <span class=\"m\">p</span> has two separate peaks (say, the heights of two different species), and we must approximate it with a single bell curve <span class=\"m\">q</span>. Minimizing the <strong>forward</strong> KL <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q)</span> forces <span class=\"m\">q</span> to put mass wherever <span class=\"m\">p</span> does, because missing any part of <span class=\"m\">p</span> is very costly. The result is a wide <span class=\"m\">q</span> that covers both peaks, even the valley between them. This is called <strong>mean-seeking</strong> or mass-covering. Minimizing the <strong>reverse</strong> KL <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(q\\,\\|\\,p)</span> instead punishes <span class=\"m\">q</span> for putting mass where <span class=\"m\">p</span> has little, so <span class=\"m\">q</span> shrinks to sit snugly on <em>one</em> peak. This is <strong>mode-seeking</strong>. Maximum likelihood training is forward KL; variational inference usually uses reverse KL.</p>\n<h3 id=\"kl-between-two-gaussians\" data-h=\"3\">KL between two Gaussians</h3>\n<p>A formula used constantly in ML: for one-dimensional Gaussians,\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}\\big(\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal N(0, 1)\\big) = \\tfrac12\\big(\\sigma^2 + \\mu^2 - 1 - \\ln\\sigma^2\\big) \\text{ nats.}</span>\nIf <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 1</span> the distributions match and the KL is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1 + 0 - 1 - 0) = 0</span>. Shifting the mean to <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1 + 1 - 1 - 0) = 0.5</span> nats. Widening to <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(4 - 1 - \\ln 4) \\approx 0.807</span> nats. This is the regularizer in every variational autoencoder.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Don't call KL a distance metric: it is not symmetric and does not satisfy the triangle inequality.</li>\n<li>Always check which distribution sits in front. In <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q)</span> the average is under <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</li>\n<li>Individual terms can be negative; only the total is guaranteed non-negative.</li>\n<li>If some <span class=\"m\">q(x) = 0</span> where <span class=\"m\">p(x) &gt; 0</span>, the answer is infinite, not a large finite number. Smooth or clip <span class=\"m\">q</span> in code.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"two-more-ways-to-read-kl\" data-h=\"3\">Two more ways to read KL</h3>\n<p>There are two other pictures of KL that are worth keeping in your head. The first is about <strong>codes</strong>. If you compress data from <span class=\"m\">p</span> using a code built for <span class=\"m\">q</span>, each symbol <span class=\"m\">x</span> gets a codeword of about <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 q(x)</span> bits instead of the ideal <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2 p(x)</span>. The waste on that symbol is <span class=\"m\">\\log_2\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</span> bits, and averaging the waste over how often each symbol really appears gives exactly <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q)</span>. For the gambler's coin, a code designed for a 90/10 coin wastes about 0.737 bits on every flip of a fair coin.</p>\n<p>The second picture is about <strong>evidence</strong>. Suppose you are trying to decide whether data comes from <span class=\"m\">p</span> or from <span class=\"m\">q</span>. Each observation <span class=\"m\">x</span> shifts the log-odds in favour of <span class=\"m\">p</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</span>. If the data really comes from <span class=\"m\">p</span>, the average shift per observation is <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q)</span>. So KL tells you how quickly evidence piles up: two distributions with a KL of 0.5 bits can be told apart in a handful of samples, while a KL of 0.001 bits needs thousands. This is why KL appears in the theory of hypothesis testing, and it is another reason the two directions differ: the evidence you collect depends on which distribution is actually producing the data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>KL divergence <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q) = \\sum p\\log\\frac pq = H(p, q) - H(p)</span> is the extra cost of believing <span class=\"m\">q</span> when the truth is <span class=\"m\">p</span>. It is never negative, is zero only when the distributions match, is not symmetric, and becomes infinite when the model rules out something possible. Forward KL covers all the mass; reverse KL locks onto a mode.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"entropy\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag p and q apart and together; the readout shows D_KL(p‖q) = H(p, q) − H(p).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Match q to p exactly. KL reads 0, the only way to get zero.\n2. Set p uniform and make q lopsided, then swap: make q uniform and p lopsided with the same shape. Compare the two KL values; they differ, showing the asymmetry.\n3. Make p4 noticeable (say 0.3) and drag q4 down to 0.01. KL climbs steeply: the model nearly rules out something that happens.\n4. Now do the reverse: make q4 large while p4 is tiny. KL grows only mildly.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> steps 3 and 4 are the \"infinite when <span class=\"m\">q(x) = 0</span>\" rule in action. Underestimating a real outcome is far worse than overestimating a rare one, which is exactly why forward KL makes models spread out to cover everything.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) = \\sum_x p(x)\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} = H(p, q) - H(p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) \\ge 0,\\quad = 0 \\iff p = q</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) \\ne D_{KL}(q\\,\\|\\,p)\\ \\text{in general}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}\\big(\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal N(0, 1)\\big) = \\tfrac12(\\sigma^2 + \\mu^2 - 1 - \\ln\\sigma^2)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Non-negativity of KL (Gibbs)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) \\ge 0</span> for all distributions <span class=\"m\">p, q</span>, with equality if and only if <span class=\"m\">p = q</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\log</span> is convex, so by Jensen's inequality (summing over <span class=\"m\">x</span> with <span class=\"m\">p(x) &gt; 0</span>)\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) = \\sum_x p(x)\\Big(-\\log\\frac{q(x)}{p(x)}\\Big) \\ge -\\log\\sum_x p(x)\\frac{q(x)}{p(x)} = -\\log\\sum_x q(x) \\ge -\\log 1 = 0.</span>\nEquality in Jensen requires <span class=\"m\">\\frac{q(x)}{p(x)}</span> to be constant, and the sums then force the constant to be 1, so <span class=\"m\">q = p</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute both directions for p = (0.5, 0.5) and q = (0.9, 0.1)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q) = 0.5\\log_2\\frac{0.5}{0.9} + 0.5\\log_2\\frac{0.5}{0.1} \\approx 0.5(-0.848) + 0.5(2.322) \\approx 0.737</span> bits.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(q\\|p) = 0.9\\log_2\\frac{0.9}{0.5} + 0.1\\log_2\\frac{0.1}{0.5} \\approx 0.763 - 0.232 = 0.531</span> bits.</li>\n<li>The two directions differ, so KL is not symmetric.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">VAE regularizer for an encoder output mean 1, standard deviation 1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Formula: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(\\sigma^2 + \\mu^2 - 1 - \\ln\\sigma^2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Plug in <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1 + 1 - 1 - 0) = 0.5</span> nats.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">\\mu = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(4 + 0 - 1 - \\ln 4) = \\frac12(3 - 1.386) \\approx 0.807</span> nats.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">KL from entropy and cross-entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Suppose <span class=\"m\">H(p) = 1.5</span> bits and <span class=\"m\">H(p, q) \\approx 1.585</span> bits (the tourist from the cross-entropy lesson).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q) = H(p, q) - H(p) \\approx 0.085</span> bits per day.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">p = (\\frac12, \\frac12)</span> and <span class=\"m\">q = (\\frac14, \\frac34)</span>. Compute <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q)</span> and <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(q\\|p)</span> in bits, and explain why they differ.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q) = \\frac12\\log_2\\frac{1/2}{1/4} + \\frac12\\log_2\\frac{1/2}{3/4} = \\frac12(1) + \\frac12\\log_2\\frac23 \\approx 0.5 - 0.292 = 0.208</span> bits.\n<span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(q\\|p) = \\frac14\\log_2\\frac{1/4}{1/2} + \\frac34\\log_2\\frac{3/4}{1/2} = \\frac14(-1) + \\frac34\\log_2 1.5 \\approx -0.25 + 0.439 = 0.189</span> bits.\nThey differ because each version averages the log-ratio under a different distribution: the first weights the outcomes equally, the second weights the second outcome three times as much.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>VAEs</strong> add <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}\\big(q(z\\mid x)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal N(0, I)\\big)</span> to the loss to keep the latent space smooth and well-behaved.</li>\n<li><strong>RLHF and PPO</strong> penalize the KL between the fine-tuned model and the original one, so fine-tuning doesn't drift into nonsense.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge distillation</strong> minimizes the KL between teacher and student output distributions.</li>\n<li><strong>Variational inference</strong> fits an approximate posterior by minimizing reverse KL, and <strong>t-SNE</strong> places points so that a KL between neighbourhood distributions is small.</li>\n<li>Minimizing cross-entropy over <span class=\"m\">q</span> is the same as minimizing <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q)</span>, since <span class=\"m\">H(p)</span> doesn't depend on the model.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In coding terms, KL is the number of extra bits per symbol you pay for compressing data from <span class=\"m\">p</span> with a code designed for <span class=\"m\">q</span>. It also measures how fast hypothesis tests can tell two sources apart: the probability of confusing them shrinks roughly like <span class=\"m\">2^{-nD_{KL}}</span> after <span class=\"m\">n</span> samples.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>Betting:</strong> a gambler who bets on horse races in proportion to their beliefs <span class=\"m\">q</span>, when the true win probabilities are <span class=\"m\">p</span>, grows their wealth more slowly than someone betting by <span class=\"m\">p</span>. The loss in long-run growth rate is exactly <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q)</span> (in bits, per race, as doublings of wealth). Wrong beliefs literally cost money at the rate given by KL.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is KL divergence symmetric?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>In general <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|q) \\ne D_{KL}(q\\|p)</span>, as the coin example showed (0.737 versus 0.531 bits).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(p\\|p)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Every log-ratio is <span class=\"m\">\\log 1 = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">H(p) = 2 bits and H(p, q) = 2.5 bits. What is D_KL(p‖q)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.5|1/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">D_{KL} = H(p, q) - H(p) = 0.5</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can a KL divergence be negative?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes, when q is better than p</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No, it is always at least 0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Only for continuous distributions</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Gibbs' inequality guarantees <span class=\"m\">D_{KL} \\ge 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What happens to D_KL(p‖q) if q(x) = 0 for some x with p(x) &gt; 0?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It becomes 0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Nothing special</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">It becomes infinite</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The term <span class=\"m\">p(x)\\log\\frac{p(x)}{0}</span> is infinite: the model ruled out something that happens.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">KL from N(1, 1) to N(0, 1), in nats, using ½(σ² + μ² − 1 − ln σ²)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.5|1/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1 + 1 - 1 - 0) = 0.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "mutual-information": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-question-behind-every-prediction\" data-h=\"3\">The question behind every prediction</h3>\n<p>Whenever we use one thing to predict another, we are betting that they share information. A thermometer reading tells you about the temperature, a symptom tells you about a disease, a pixel tells you about the pixel next to it, and a caption tells you about an image. <strong>Mutual information</strong> measures exactly how much: the number of bits that knowing one variable saves you, on average, in describing the other. It is symmetric, it catches any kind of relationship (not just straight-line ones), and it sets the speed limit for every communication system ever built.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Go back to the train station from the joint entropy lesson. Before looking outside, your uncertainty about whether the next person carries an umbrella was <span class=\"m\">H(Y) \\approx 0.934</span> bits. After seeing the weather, it dropped on average to <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) \\approx 0.722</span> bits. The weather \"told you\" about <span class=\"m\">0.212</span> bits about umbrellas. That number is the mutual information <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span> between weather and umbrellas. Remarkably, if you had instead watched umbrellas and asked about the weather, you would have learned exactly the same <span class=\"m\">0.212</span> bits about the weather. Information is shared equally in both directions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>The <strong>mutual information</strong> between <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is the reduction in uncertainty about one from learning the other:\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = H(X) - H(X \\mid Y) = H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X).</span>\nUsing the chain rule <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X)</span>, we can also write it without conditionals:\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X, Y).</span>\nThis form explains the symmetry: it treats <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> identically. Picture two overlapping discs, one for <span class=\"m\">H(X)</span> and one for <span class=\"m\">H(Y)</span>. Their union is <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y)</span>, the parts that don't overlap are the conditional entropies, and the overlap is <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span>.</p>\n<p>A third form is the one most useful in theory:\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = \\sum_{x, y}p(x, y)\\log\\frac{p(x, y)}{p(x)p(y)} = D_{KL}\\big(p(x, y)\\,\\|\\,p(x)p(y)\\big).</span>\nIt compares the real joint distribution with what the joint would be if the variables were independent. The more the truth differs from independence, the more information is shared.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mutual information</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = H(X) - H(X \\mid Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X, Y) = D_{KL}(p(x, y)\\,\\|\\,p(x)p(y))</span>: the average number of bits that observing one variable tells you about the other.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-worked-build-up\" data-h=\"3\">A worked build-up</h3>\n<p>Take two fair bits with the joint table <span class=\"m\">p(0,0) = 0.4</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(0,1) = 0.1</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1,0) = 0.1</span>, <span class=\"m\">p(1,1) = 0.4</span>. They agree 80% of the time. Each marginal is <span class=\"m\">(\\frac12, \\frac12)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(X) = H(Y) = 1</span> bit. The joint entropy is <span class=\"m\">-2(0.4\\log_2 0.4) - 2(0.1\\log_2 0.1) \\approx 1.058 + 0.664 = 1.722</span> bits. So\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = 1 + 1 - 1.722 = 0.278 \\text{ bits.}</span>\nIf they agreed 100% of the time, <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y)</span> would be 1 and <span class=\"m\">I = 1</span> bit: knowing one tells you the other completely. If they were independent, <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">I = 0</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"key-properties\" data-h=\"3\">Key properties</h3>\n<p>Mutual information is always <strong>non-negative</strong>, because it is a KL divergence. It is <strong>zero exactly when <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent</strong>, because only then is <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p(x)p(y)</span> everywhere. It is <strong>symmetric</strong>: <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = I(Y; X)</span>. And a variable shares all of its information with itself: <span class=\"m\">I(X; X) = H(X)</span>, which is why entropy is sometimes called \"self-information\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"better-than-correlation\" data-h=\"3\">Better than correlation</h3>\n<p>Correlation only detects <strong>straight-line</strong> relationships. Mutual information detects <strong>any</strong> dependence. Here is the classic example. Let <span class=\"m\">X</span> be <span class=\"m\">-1</span>, <span class=\"m\">0</span> or <span class=\"m\">1</span> with equal probability, and let <span class=\"m\">Y = X^2</span>. Clearly <span class=\"m\">Y</span> depends on <span class=\"m\">X</span>: once you know <span class=\"m\">X</span> you know <span class=\"m\">Y</span> exactly. But the correlation is zero: <span class=\"m\">E[X] = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = E[X^3] = \\frac13(-1 + 0 + 1) = 0</span>, so the covariance is <span class=\"m\">0</span>. Mutual information is not fooled: since <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is determined by <span class=\"m\">X</span>, <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = H(Y)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is 1 with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac23</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I = h(\\frac23) \\approx 0.918</span> bits.</p>\n<h3 id=\"processing-can-t-create-information\" data-h=\"3\">Processing can't create information</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">X</span> affects <span class=\"m\">Y</span>, and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> alone affects <span class=\"m\">Z</span> (a chain <span class=\"m\">X \\to Y \\to Z</span>), then\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Z) \\le I(X; Y).</span>\nThis is the <strong>data processing inequality</strong>. No amount of clever computation on <span class=\"m\">Y</span> can tell you more about <span class=\"m\">X</span> than <span class=\"m\">Y</span> itself contained. Every layer of a neural network is such a processing step, so deeper layers can only keep or lose information about the input; what they can do is reorganize it into a more useful form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"channel-capacity\" data-h=\"3\">Channel capacity</h3>\n<p>Shannon's most famous application is communication. A <strong>channel</strong> takes an input <span class=\"m\">X</span> and produces a noisy output <span class=\"m\">Y</span>. The simplest is the <strong>binary symmetric channel</strong>: each bit is flipped with probability <span class=\"m\">f</span>. Its <strong>capacity</strong> is the most mutual information you can get between input and output:\n<span class=\"mb\">C = \\max_{p(x)} I(X; Y) = 1 - h(f) \\text{ bits per use,}</span>\nreached with fair input bits. With <span class=\"m\">f = 0.1</span>, <span class=\"m\">C = 1 - 0.469 = 0.531</span> bits per use. Shannon's <strong>noisy-channel coding theorem</strong> says that you can send data with as few errors as you like at any rate below <span class=\"m\">C</span>, and not above it. With <span class=\"m\">f = 0.5</span> the output is pure noise and <span class=\"m\">C = 0</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Channel capacity</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The maximum of <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span> over input distributions: the highest rate, in bits per channel use, at which information can be sent with arbitrarily small error.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Zero correlation does <strong>not</strong> mean zero mutual information; <span class=\"m\">Y = X^2</span> is the counterexample.</li>\n<li>Mutual information is a property of the joint distribution, not of a single pair of values.</li>\n<li>Don't confuse <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">H(X, Y)</span>. The first is the overlap; the second is the union.</li>\n<li>Estimating mutual information from a small sample is hard and biased upward; use enough data or a dedicated estimator.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"conditional-mutual-information\" data-h=\"3\">Conditional mutual information</h3>\n<p>Sometimes we want to know what <span class=\"m\">Y</span> tells us about <span class=\"m\">X</span> <strong>after</strong> we already know a third variable <span class=\"m\">Z</span>. That is the <strong>conditional mutual information</strong> <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y \\mid Z) = H(X \\mid Z) - H(X \\mid Y, Z)</span>. It can be smaller or larger than <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span>. If ice-cream sales and drownings are both driven by hot weather <span class=\"m\">Z</span>, they share information on their own, but once you condition on the temperature the shared information mostly disappears: <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y \\mid Z) \\approx 0</span>. On the other hand, if <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are two independent fair bits and <span class=\"m\">Z = X \\oplus Y</span> is their exclusive or, then <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = 0</span> but <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y \\mid Z) = 1</span> bit, because once you know <span class=\"m\">Z</span>, learning <span class=\"m\">Y</span> tells you <span class=\"m\">X</span> exactly. Conditioning can both hide and reveal dependence, which is why careful causal reasoning always asks \"conditional on what?\". The chain rule ties these together: <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y, Z) = I(X; Z) + I(X; Y \\mid Z)</span>, so the information two clues give together is the information of the first plus whatever new information the second adds.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Mutual information <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X, Y)</span> is the overlap of two uncertainties: how many bits one variable tells you about the other. It is symmetric, non-negative, zero only under independence, and it detects nonlinear dependence that correlation misses. Processing can't increase it, and its maximum over inputs is the capacity of a channel.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g07-joint\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Use the presets to see mutual information as the overlap of H(X) and H(Y).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Click <strong>independent</strong>: the green overlap vanishes and <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = 0</span>.\n2. Click <strong>Y is a copy of X</strong>: the bars coincide and <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = H(X) = 1</span> bit.\n3. Set the sliders to 0.4, 0.1, 0.1, 0.4 and confirm <span class=\"m\">I \\approx 0.278</span> bits from the article.\n4. Click <strong>noisy copy</strong> (flip probability 0.1) and read <span class=\"m\">I \\approx 0.531</span> bits, the capacity of that binary symmetric channel.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the green box is the same whether you think of it as \"<span class=\"m\">H(X)</span> minus what's left of <span class=\"m\">X</span>\" or \"<span class=\"m\">H(Y)</span> minus what's left of <span class=\"m\">Y</span>\". That is the symmetry of mutual information, drawn as one shared overlap.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"scatter-corr\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Correlation sees only straight-line patterns.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set ρ to 0 and notice the cloud looks shapeless: here X and Y really are independent, so mutual information is 0 too.\n2. Raise ρ to 0.9: the points line up and both correlation and mutual information are large.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> this widget can only make linear relationships. A U-shaped cloud like <span class=\"m\">Y = X^2</span> would also show <span class=\"m\">r \\approx 0</span>, yet it has high mutual information. Correlation measures one kind of dependence; mutual information measures all of them.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = H(X) - H(X \\mid Y) = H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X, Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = D_{KL}\\big(p(x, y)\\,\\|\\,p(x)p(y)\\big) \\ge 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; X) = H(X)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">C_{\\text{BSC}} = 1 - h(f)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Independence and mutual information</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) \\ge 0</span>, with equality if and only if <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are independent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = D_{KL}(p(x, y)\\,\\|\\,p(x)p(y))</span>, and KL divergence is non-negative and zero exactly when the two distributions are equal, which here means <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p(x)p(y)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">x, y</span>: the definition of independence.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Data processing inequality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">X \\to Y \\to Z</span> is a Markov chain (<span class=\"m\">Z</span> depends on <span class=\"m\">X</span> only through <span class=\"m\">Y</span>), then <span class=\"m\">I(X; Z) \\le I(X; Y)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the chain rule for mutual information, <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y, Z) = I(X; Z) + I(X; Y \\mid Z) = I(X; Y) + I(X; Z \\mid Y)</span>. The Markov property makes <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Z</span> independent given <span class=\"m\">Y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I(X; Z \\mid Y) = 0</span>. Hence <span class=\"m\">I(X; Z) = I(X; Y) - I(X; Y \\mid Z) \\le I(X; Y)</span>, since conditional mutual information is non-negative.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Noisy-channel coding theorem (Shannon, 1948)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a channel with capacity <span class=\"m\">C</span>, every rate <span class=\"m\">R &lt; C</span> can be achieved with error probability as small as desired by using long enough codes, and no rate <span class=\"m\">R &gt; C</span> can.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mutual information of the table (0.4, 0.1; 0.1, 0.4)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Marginals are <span class=\"m\">(\\frac12, \\frac12)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(X) = H(Y) = 1</span> bit.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H(X, Y) = -2(0.4)\\log_2 0.4 - 2(0.1)\\log_2 0.1 \\approx 0.8(1.322) + 0.2(3.322) \\approx 1.722</span> bits.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">I = 1 + 1 - 1.722 \\approx 0.278</span> bits. (Equivalently <span class=\"m\">1 - h(0.2)</span>, since <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is <span class=\"m\">X</span> flipped with probability 0.2.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Y = X² with X uniform on {−1, 0, 1}: correlation versus mutual information</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E[X] = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">E[XY] = E[X^3] = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\text{Cov}(X, Y) = 0</span> and the correlation is 0.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">Y</span> is a function of <span class=\"m\">X</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = H(Y)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P(Y = 1) = \\frac23</span>, <span class=\"m\">P(Y = 0) = \\frac13</span>, so <span class=\"m\">I = h(\\frac13) \\approx 0.918</span> bits. Strong dependence, zero correlation.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Capacity of a binary symmetric channel with f = 0.1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h(0.1) \\approx 0.469</span> bits.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">C = 1 - 0.469 = 0.531</span> bits per use.</li>\n<li>To send 1000 data bits reliably you need at least about <span class=\"m\">1000/0.531 \\approx 1884</span> channel uses.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A fair bit <span class=\"m\">X</span> is sent through a channel that flips it with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y)</span> and the minimum number of channel uses per data bit for reliable communication.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">Y</span> is also a fair bit, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y) = 1</span>. Given <span class=\"m\">X</span>, <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is wrong with probability <span class=\"m\">\\frac14</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H(Y \\mid X) = h(\\frac14) = \\frac14\\log_2 4 + \\frac34\\log_2\\frac43 \\approx 0.5 + 0.311 = 0.811</span> bits. Hence <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = 1 - 0.811 = 0.189</span> bits, which is the capacity since fair inputs are optimal here. Reliable communication needs at least <span class=\"m\">1/0.189 \\approx 5.3</span> channel uses per data bit.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Feature selection:</strong> rank features by <span class=\"m\">I(\\text{feature}; \\text{label})</span>; unlike correlation, this finds nonlinear signals.</li>\n<li><strong>Contrastive learning</strong> (CLIP, SimCLR) trains with the InfoNCE loss, which maximizes a lower bound on the mutual information between two views of the same thing, such as an image and its caption.</li>\n<li><strong>Information bottleneck:</strong> good representations keep the information about the label while discarding the rest of the input.</li>\n<li>Decision-tree information gain is exactly <span class=\"m\">I(\\text{feature}; \\text{label})</span>.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Channel capacity is the foundation of digital communication. Error-correcting codes (Hamming, Reed–Solomon, LDPC, turbo and polar codes) are designed to approach Shannon's limit; modern LDPC and polar codes in Wi-Fi and 5G get very close to it. QR codes and CDs use Reed–Solomon codes so scratched or smudged data can still be read.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Mutual information is used to align medical images: a CT scan and an MRI of the same patient look completely different pixel by pixel, but when the images are correctly lined up the mutual information between their intensities is maximal. Registration software slides and rotates one image to maximize it.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If X and Y are independent, I(X;Y) =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Independence means <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = p(x)p(y)</span>, so the KL form is 0.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">X is a fair coin. What is I(X; X) in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">I(X; X) = H(X) = 1</span> bit.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">H(X) = 1 bit and H(X | Y) = 0.25 bits. What is I(X; Y)?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.75|3/4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">I = H(X) - H(X \\mid Y) = 0.75</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A binary symmetric channel flips bits with probability 0.5. What is its capacity?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 - h(0.5) = 1 - 1 = 0</span>: the output is independent of the input.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Zero correlation between X and Y implies:</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">I(X;Y) = 0</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Nothing about I(X;Y) in general; it can still be large</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">X and Y are independent</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">Y = X^2</span> with symmetric <span class=\"m\">X</span> has zero correlation but <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = H(Y) &gt; 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which is true for a chain X → Y → Z?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">I(X;Z) ≤ I(X;Y)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">I(X;Z) ≥ I(X;Y)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">I(X;Z) = H(X)</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The data processing inequality: processing <span class=\"m\">Y</span> can't add information about <span class=\"m\">X</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "differential-entropy": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-continuous-variables-need-care\" data-h=\"3\">Why continuous variables need care</h3>\n<p>Heights, temperatures, pixel intensities and neural-network weights are continuous: they can take any value in a range, not just a list of options. We'd like the same tools (entropy, cross-entropy, KL) for them. The natural idea is to replace the sum with an integral and the probabilities with a <strong>density</strong> <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span>. That gives <strong>differential entropy</strong>, which behaves like entropy in most ways but has a couple of surprises you need to know about. It is also the bridge that explains one of the most important facts in machine learning: training with mean squared error is secretly minimizing a cross-entropy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine measuring the time a bus arrives, uniformly at random within a 10-minute window. If you recorded the time to the nearest minute, there would be 10 equally likely values and an entropy of <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 10 \\approx 3.32</span> bits. To the nearest 6 seconds, 100 values and <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 100 \\approx 6.64</span> bits. To the nearest millisecond, even more. The exact arrival time, specified to infinite precision, carries infinitely many bits. So the \"plain\" entropy of a continuous variable is infinite, and we need a different measure that ignores the precision we choose and captures only the shape of the distribution.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>For a continuous random variable with density <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span>, the <strong>differential entropy</strong> is\n<span class=\"mb\">h(X) = -\\int f(x)\\log f(x)\\,dx.</span>\nLet's compute it for a uniform distribution on <span class=\"m\">[0, a]</span>, where <span class=\"m\">f(x) = \\frac1a</span> on the interval. Then\n<span class=\"mb\">h(X) = -\\int_0^a \\frac1a\\log_2\\frac1a\\,dx = \\log_2 a \\text{ bits.}</span>\nFor the bus window, <span class=\"m\">a = 10</span> minutes, so <span class=\"m\">h = \\log_2 10 \\approx 3.32</span> bits, which matches the \"nearest minute\" count. That is no accident: if you round a continuous variable into bins of width <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span>, its discrete entropy is approximately <span class=\"m\">h(X) - \\log_2\\Delta</span>. Differential entropy is the part that depends on the distribution; the <span class=\"m\">-\\log_2\\Delta</span> part depends only on your chosen precision. With <span class=\"m\">\\Delta = 0.1</span> minutes we add <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 10</span> bits, giving <span class=\"m\">6.64</span> as before.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Differential entropy</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a density <span class=\"m\">f</span>: <span class=\"m\">h(X) = -\\int f(x)\\log f(x)\\,dx</span>. It measures the spread of a continuous distribution; unlike discrete entropy it can be negative and it changes when you rescale the variable.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"two-surprises\" data-h=\"3\">Two surprises</h3>\n<p>First, differential entropy <strong>can be negative</strong>. A uniform distribution on <span class=\"m\">[0, \\frac14]</span> has <span class=\"m\">h = \\log_2\\frac14 = -2</span> bits. That doesn't mean \"negative information\"; it means the distribution is narrower than a uniform on an interval of length 1, which is the reference point with <span class=\"m\">h = 0</span>. Second, it <strong>depends on units</strong>. Measuring in seconds instead of minutes multiplies every value by 60 and adds <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 60</span> bits. In general <span class=\"m\">h(cX) = h(X) + \\log_2|c|</span>. Differences between differential entropies, and KL divergences, don't have this problem, which is why KL and mutual information are the quantities used most for continuous variables.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-gaussian\" data-h=\"3\">The Gaussian</h3>\n<p>For a normal distribution <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span> the integral works out to\n<span class=\"mb\">h(X) = \\tfrac12\\log_2(2\\pi e\\sigma^2) \\text{ bits} = \\tfrac12\\ln(2\\pi e\\sigma^2) \\text{ nats.}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">2\\pi e \\approx 17.08</span>, so <span class=\"m\">h \\approx \\frac12\\log_2 17.08 \\approx 2.047</span> bits, or about <span class=\"m\">1.419</span> nats. The mean doesn't appear: sliding a distribution left or right doesn't change its spread. Doubling <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> adds exactly 1 bit, consistent with the rescaling rule.</p>\n<p>The Gaussian has a special status: <strong>among all distributions with a given variance, the Gaussian has the largest differential entropy.</strong> It is the \"most random\" or least-assuming distribution once you fix the spread. This is one reason Gaussians are the default noise model: assuming Gaussian noise means assuming nothing beyond the variance.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-mean-squared-error-is-a-cross-entropy\" data-h=\"3\">Why mean squared error is a cross-entropy</h3>\n<p>Suppose a regression model predicts <span class=\"m\">\\hat y</span> and we assume the true <span class=\"m\">y</span> is Gaussian around the prediction, <span class=\"m\">y \\sim \\mathcal N(\\hat y, \\sigma^2)</span>. The negative log-likelihood of one observation is\n<span class=\"mb\">-\\ln f(y) = \\frac{(y - \\hat y)^2}{2\\sigma^2} + \\frac12\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2).</span>\nThe second term is a constant. So minimizing the average negative log-likelihood, which is the cross-entropy between the data and the model, is exactly minimizing the <strong>mean squared error</strong>. For example with <span class=\"m\">y = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 2.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.25}{2} + 0.919 \\approx 1.044</span> nats. Classification uses categorical cross-entropy; regression with MSE uses Gaussian cross-entropy. They are the same principle.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li>Don't read a negative differential entropy as an error; it is normal for narrow distributions.</li>\n<li>Don't compare differential entropies measured in different units.</li>\n<li>A density <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span> can exceed 1, so <span class=\"m\">-\\log f(x)</span> can be negative; it is not a probability.</li>\n<li>Discrete entropy is the limit of differential entropy only after subtracting the <span class=\"m\">\\log_2\\Delta</span> term; the two are not the same number.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"mutual-information-carries-over-cleanly\" data-h=\"3\">Mutual information carries over cleanly</h3>\n<p>The good news is that the quantities built from <strong>differences</strong> behave exactly as in the discrete case. The mutual information of continuous variables is <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = h(X) - h(X \\mid Y)</span>, and the <span class=\"m\">\\log_2\\Delta</span> precision terms cancel when you subtract, so it is always non-negative, does not depend on units, and is zero exactly under independence. For two jointly Gaussian variables with correlation <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span> there is a neat closed form: <span class=\"m\">I(X; Y) = -\\frac12\\log_2(1 - \\rho^2)</span> bits. With <span class=\"m\">\\rho = 0.6</span>, <span class=\"m\">1 - \\rho^2 = 0.64</span> and <span class=\"m\">I = -\\frac12\\log_2 0.64 \\approx 0.322</span> bits. As <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span> approaches 1 the mutual information grows without bound, because a perfectly correlated continuous variable pins down the other to infinite precision. The KL divergence between densities, <span class=\"m\">\\int f\\log\\frac fg</span>, is likewise non-negative and unit-free, which is why it is the workhorse of continuous probabilistic machine learning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Differential entropy <span class=\"m\">h(X) = -\\int f\\log f</span> extends entropy to continuous variables. A uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, a]</span> has <span class=\"m\">h = \\log_2 a</span>, which can be negative; a Gaussian has <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\log_2(2\\pi e\\sigma^2)</span> and is the maximum-entropy distribution for its variance. KL divergence and mutual information carry over cleanly, and the Gaussian negative log-likelihood turns cross-entropy into mean squared error.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"distribution\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;normal&quot;, &quot;lo&quot;: -1, &quot;hi&quot;: 1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Change the standard deviation and imagine its entropy: wider curve, more bits.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Read the σ slider. Use <span class=\"m\">h = 2.047 + \\log_2\\sigma</span> bits to compute the entropy.\n2. Double σ, from 1 to 2. The curve gets wider and lower; the entropy rises by exactly 1 bit.\n3. Drag σ down to its minimum, 0.3. The peak rises to about 1.33 and <span class=\"m\">h \\approx 2.047 + \\log_2 0.3 \\approx 0.31</span> bits, close to zero. Any σ below about 0.242 would make <span class=\"m\">h</span> negative.\n4. Switch the Distribution menu to uniform and compare its shape with the bell curve of the same spread.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the μ slider only slides the curve and never changes the entropy, while the spread controls everything. For a fixed spread, no shape has more entropy than the bell curve.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">h(X) = -\\int f(x)\\log f(x)\\,dx</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(\\text{Uniform}[0, a]) = \\log_2 a</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)) = \\tfrac12\\log_2(2\\pi e\\sigma^2)\\ \\text{bits}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(cX) = h(X) + \\log_2|c|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(\\mathcal N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)\\|\\mathcal N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)) = \\ln\\frac{\\sigma_2}{\\sigma_1} + \\frac{\\sigma_1^2 + (\\mu_1 - \\mu_2)^2}{2\\sigma_2^2} - \\frac12</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">-\\ln\\mathcal N(y; \\hat y, \\sigma^2) = \\frac{(y - \\hat y)^2}{2\\sigma^2} + \\tfrac12\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Maximum entropy of the Gaussian</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Among all densities with variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span>, the normal distribution <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)</span> has the largest differential entropy, <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\ln(2\\pi e\\sigma^2)</span> nats.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f</span> have variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span> (and, without loss of generality, mean <span class=\"m\">\\mu</span>), and let <span class=\"m\">g</span> be the Gaussian with the same mean and variance. Then <span class=\"m\">0 \\le D_{KL}(f\\|g) = -h(f) - \\int f\\ln g</span>. Now <span class=\"m\">-\\ln g(x) = \\frac12\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2) + \\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}</span>, and its average under <span class=\"m\">f</span> uses only the variance of <span class=\"m\">f</span>, which equals that of <span class=\"m\">g</span>: <span class=\"m\">-\\int f\\ln g = \\frac12\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2) + \\frac12 = h(g)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">h(f) \\le h(g)</span>, with equality only when <span class=\"m\">f = g</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Entropy of a uniform distribution on [0, 8] and on [0, 1/4]</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h = \\log_2 a</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">[0, 8]</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 8 = 3</span> bits.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">[0, \\frac14]</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\log_2\\frac14 = -2</span> bits. Negative, because the interval is shorter than 1.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Entropy of N(0, 4) in nats</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">2\\pi e\\sigma^2 \\approx 17.08 \\times 4 = 68.3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">h = \\frac12\\ln 68.3 \\approx \\frac12(4.224) \\approx 2.112</span> nats.</li>\n<li>Check: <span class=\"m\">1.419 + \\ln 2 \\approx 1.419 + 0.693 = 2.112</span>, using the rescaling rule.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gaussian negative log-likelihood for y = 3, prediction 2.5, σ = 1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Squared-error part: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{(3 - 2.5)^2}{2} = 0.125</span>.</li>\n<li>Constant part: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\ln(2\\pi) \\approx 0.919</span>.</li>\n<li>NLL <span class=\"m\">\\approx 1.044</span> nats. Only the first part depends on the prediction, so minimizing NLL is minimizing squared error.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compute <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(\\mathcal N(0, 1)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal N(0, 4))</span> and <span class=\"m\">D_{KL}(\\mathcal N(0, 4)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal N(0, 1))</span> in nats. Which is larger, and why?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Using the formula with <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2 = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\ln 2 + \\frac{1}{8} - \\frac12 \\approx 0.693 - 0.375 = 0.318</span> nats. Reversed, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma_2 = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\ln\\frac12 + \\frac42 - \\frac12 \\approx -0.693 + 1.5 = 0.807</span> nats. The second is larger: approximating a wide distribution with a narrow one assigns tiny density to values that really happen, which KL punishes heavily; a wide model covering a narrow truth only wastes some density.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ul>\n<li><strong>Regression losses:</strong> MSE is Gaussian negative log-likelihood, and mean absolute error is Laplace negative log-likelihood. Choosing a loss is choosing a noise model.</li>\n<li><strong>VAEs and diffusion models</strong> use closed-form Gaussian KL terms and Gaussian likelihoods throughout.</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum entropy reinforcement learning</strong> maximizes the differential entropy of a continuous action policy, often Gaussian, to encourage exploration.</li>\n</ul></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lossy compression of audio and images is governed by rate–distortion theory, built on differential entropy: for a Gaussian source of variance <span class=\"m\">\\sigma^2</span> coded with mean squared error <span class=\"m\">D</span>, the minimum rate is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\log_2\\frac{\\sigma^2}{D}</span> bits per sample. Each extra bit halves the allowed standard deviation of the error.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Shannon–Hartley formula for a telephone line, Wi-Fi link or any channel with Gaussian noise, <span class=\"m\">C = B\\log_2(1 + \\frac{S}{N})</span>, comes from differential entropy. A 1 MHz channel with signal-to-noise ratio 1000 (30 dB) can carry at most about <span class=\"m\">10^6\\log_2 1001 \\approx 10</span> million bits per second.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Differential entropy of a uniform distribution on [0, 4], in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\log_2 4 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Can differential entropy be negative?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, \\frac14]</span> has <span class=\"m\">h = -2</span> bits.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which distribution has the largest differential entropy for a fixed variance?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Uniform</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Gaussian</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Exponential</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The maximum entropy theorem: the Gaussian.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Doubling the standard deviation of a Gaussian adds how many bits of differential entropy?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">h(2X) = h(X) + \\log_2 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Differential entropy of Uniform[0, 1] in bits?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\log_2 1 = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Minimizing mean squared error corresponds to maximum likelihood under which noise model?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Gaussian</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Bernoulli</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Uniform</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The Gaussian NLL is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{(y - \\hat y)^2}{2\\sigma^2}</span> plus a constant.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "surprise", "t": "Information and surprise", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">I(p) = -\\log_2 p\\ \\text{bits} = -\\ln p\\ \\text{nats}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(pq) = I(p) + I(q)\\quad\\text{for independent events}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">1\\ \\text{bit} = \\ln 2 \\approx 0.693\\ \\text{nats}</span></p>"}, {"l": "entropy", "t": "Entropy", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">H(X) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log_2 p(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">0 \\le H(X) \\le \\log_2 n,\\quad H = \\log_2 n \\iff X \\text{ uniform}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(p) = -p\\log_2 p - (1-p)\\log_2(1-p)</span></p>"}, {"l": "joint-conditional-entropy", "t": "Joint entropy, conditional entropy and information gain", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) = -\\sum_{x, y}p(x, y)\\log_2 p(x, y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(Y \\mid X) = \\sum_x p(x)H(Y \\mid X = x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X, Y) = H(X) + H(Y \\mid X) = H(Y) + H(X \\mid Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">0 \\le H(Y \\mid X) \\le H(Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">IG = H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X)</span></p>"}, {"l": "source-coding", "t": "Codes and compression", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\bar L = \\sum_i p_i\\ell_i</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sum_i 2^{-\\ell_i} \\le 1 \\quad\\text{(Kraft)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\ell_i^{\\ast} = -\\log_2 p_i,\\qquad \\ell_i = \\lceil -\\log_2 p_i\\rceil</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(X) \\le \\bar L_{\\text{Huffman}} &lt; H(X) + 1</span></p>"}, {"l": "cross-entropy", "t": "Cross-entropy", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) = -\\sum_x p(x)\\log q(x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H(p, q) \\ge H(p),\\quad H(p, q) = H(p) + D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L_{\\text{CE}} = -\\log q_y,\\qquad L_{\\text{BCE}} = -[y\\log\\hat y + (1-y)\\log(1-\\hat y)]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z_i} = q_i - p_i \\quad\\text{(softmax + cross-entropy)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{perplexity} = 2^{H_{\\text{bits}}} = e^{H_{\\text{nats}}}</span></p>"}, {"l": "kl", "t": "KL divergence", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) = \\sum_x p(x)\\log\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} = H(p, q) - H(p)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) \\ge 0,\\quad = 0 \\iff p = q</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(p\\,\\|\\,q) \\ne D_{KL}(q\\,\\|\\,p)\\ \\text{in general}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}\\big(\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)\\,\\|\\,\\mathcal N(0, 1)\\big) = \\tfrac12(\\sigma^2 + \\mu^2 - 1 - \\ln\\sigma^2)</span></p>"}, {"l": "mutual-information", "t": "Mutual information and channel capacity", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = H(X) - H(X \\mid Y) = H(Y) - H(Y \\mid X)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = H(X) + H(Y) - H(X, Y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; Y) = D_{KL}\\big(p(x, y)\\,\\|\\,p(x)p(y)\\big) \\ge 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">I(X; X) = H(X)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">C_{\\text{BSC}} = 1 - h(f)</span></p>"}, {"l": "differential-entropy", "t": "Differential entropy and continuous variables", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">h(X) = -\\int f(x)\\log f(x)\\,dx</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(\\text{Uniform}[0, a]) = \\log_2 a</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(\\mathcal N(\\mu, \\sigma^2)) = \\tfrac12\\log_2(2\\pi e\\sigma^2)\\ \\text{bits}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">h(cX) = h(X) + \\log_2|c|</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{KL}(\\mathcal N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)\\|\\mathcal N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)) = \\ln\\frac{\\sigma_2}{\\sigma_1} + \\frac{\\sigma_1^2 + (\\mu_1 - \\mu_2)^2}{2\\sigma_2^2} - \\frac12</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">-\\ln\\mathcal N(y; \\hat y, \\sigma^2) = \\frac{(y - \\hat y)^2}{2\\sigma^2} + \\tfrac12\\ln(2\\pi\\sigma^2)</span></p>"}]});
